CG.CONTENT = {"articles":[],"rulebook":{"chapters":[{"num":0,"title":"How our season works — and why","sections":[{"id":"0.1","title":"Reading this chapter","paragraphs":["This chapter describes how a CGHL season is conducted, in order, together with the purpose of each stage. It is intended to be read in full before a member's first game. The binding rules are set out in the chapters that follow; where a stage described here carries a requirement, the rule that governs it is cited.","The league conducts each season under one of two season formats, designated by the commissioner for that season and stated at the head of this rulebook. The basic format is the league standard and is the text of these chapters: a season with no pre-season, a single draft night, six (6) game-weeks, a three-round postseason, and every player returned to the draft pool at its end. The full format is the more extensive model — a pre-season, a free-agency period, multi-season contracts, contract extensions and the trading of draft picks — held in reserve for a larger league; its provisions are preserved in full in Appendix A and are not in force while the basic format is designated. Where the two formats differ, the chapters that follow state the provision in force and the appendix carries the alternative.","Three figures are season settings rather than fixed provisions of this rulebook: the size and composition of the active roster (Rule 2.1), the number of rounds in the entry draft (Rule 2.8) and the number of regular-season games a player needs to be dressed in the playoffs (Rule 8.3). The commissioner determines each for every season and publishes them to the clubs before that season's draft; once published for a season they are binding for its duration. The figures in force appear on the site — on the Seasons page, in the draft room and in Team HQ — and the office announces them when they are set.","The calendar is spaced from a single anchor, draft night, and every later date follows from it, so that the dates are internally consistent and every club knows what follows."]},{"id":"0.2","title":"Step one — the off-season","paragraphs":["The basic format has no formal off-season. Registration for a season opens automatically when the previous season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), and the interval between a season's final and the next season's draft is used by the league office to renew or appoint each club's Owner and by those Owners to seat their General Manager and Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6). No club may draft until all three seats are held (Rule 2.8).","The interval is short by design. Because every roster is rebuilt through the draft and no player contract carries from one season to the next, the only precondition for a new season is that each club's front office is in place."],"full":["The off-season begins at the first midnight after the previous season's final playoff game and runs two full weeks with no games on the ice. These two weeks exist for the league office to renew or appoint each club's Owner, and for those Owners to set their management staff, before the pre-season opens — next-season sign-ups have been open since the previous season’s movement deadline (Rule 1.1).","Why it comes first: rosters are rebuilt every season through the draft and the contracts that carry over — including extensions a club signed during its players' final season (Rule 2.5) — so the people who will run the draft — the Owners and their managers — have to be in place before anyone signs up. The two dark weeks give the office room to seat clubs deliberately rather than under game-night pressure."]},{"id":"0.3","title":"Step two — registration and the eligibility deadline","paragraphs":["Registration for a season opens when the previous season's movement deadline passes (Rule 1.1); for the league's inaugural season it opened at launch. The sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft — is a draft-eligibility cutoff and not a closure of registration. A player registered by the cutoff enters the draft. A player who registers after it, at any point in the season, remains eligible to play and is placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8 rather than drafted.","Registration remains open so that a player who discovers the league late is not excluded. Automatic placement under Rule 2.8 assigns each late registrant to a club as depth, at the league minimum salary, until placement closes at the movement deadline (Rule 2.4). A player who registers after the movement deadline enters the following season's pool.","Until a season's registration ends, a registered player may withdraw his own registration from the Register page (Rule 1.1); a registered player who leaves the league Discord is withdrawn automatically after one day's grace (Rule 1.1). A player who has been placed on a club leaves the season only through the league office.","The position declared at registration governs the position at which a player is drafted and, if undrafted, placed. It may be changed only by request to the league office filed before the sign-up cutoff (Rule 2.9); after the cutoff it is fixed for the season."],"full":["Registration for a season opens the moment the previous season’s movement deadline passes (Rule 1.1) — for the league’s first season, during the off-season. The date the league calls the sign-up deadline — 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft — is a draft-eligibility cutoff, not a hard close: register on time and you enter the pre-season and the draft as a normal prospect; register after it — or join at any point later in the season — and you still play, you are simply placed on a club automatically rather than drafted.","Why it works this way: a league that slams registration shut turns away players who find it a week late. Registration stays open, and automatic placement under Rule 2.2 routes an unrostered arrival onto a club that has room, at the league minimum — up until random placement closes at the movement deadline (Rule 2.4). Register after that and you enter the next season’s pool.","The deadline has one more job: it is the measuring date for contracts. A player on a multi-year deal must still register — every season, without exception. Sign up and the contract puts the player straight back on the club; fail to sign up and the salary sits on the club's cap as dead money while the player sits out — and if the club has changed owners, the deal is voided and the player is suspended for its remaining seasons (Rule 2.5).","Until a season's registration ends, a registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page (Rule 1.1); leaving the league Discord does the same thing automatically after a day's grace (Rule 1.1). Once placed on a club, leaving goes through the league office.","One thing to get right at sign-up: the position you register at. It decides which club you are assigned to for the pre-season and how clubs value you in the draft, and it can only be changed by request to the league office up to 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game (Rule 2.9). After that it is set for the season."]},{"id":"0.4","title":"Step three — draft week","paragraphs":["The basic format has no pre-season. No player is assigned to a club before the draft, no appearance requirement applies, and the interval between the sign-up cutoff and draft night is used by club management to prepare draft boards against the published pool (Rule 2.8) and by the league office to settle the pool. Every player registered by the cutoff is draft-eligible without further condition.","A pre-season occupies two game-weeks and conditions draft eligibility on an appearance count. In a developing league the season is better served by drafting every registered player on the published Saturday and opening the regular season on the following Wednesday. Overall ratings are compiled from regular-season play, so no information the pre-season provided is lost."],"full":["The pre-season opens on the first Wednesday after the two dark weeks end and runs two weeks; sign-ups stay open right through it, until 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft (Rule 1.1). A club’s management group plays for its own club, and in pre-season games its Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position, so that a club can fill its bench and get as many players into games as possible (Rule 5.2). Every other registered player is assigned randomly and evenly across all clubs for two weeks of real games that carry their own standings, separate from the regular season. The assignment fills the clubs' active rosters first, by position group (Rule 2.1): a player keeps his registered position and goes to a club with room in his group — a club short of left defensemen takes an extra right defenseman, a club short of centers an extra wing — the emptiest groups first; when no club has room in his group, he is loaned into another group's open seat — a forward may spend the pre-season on defense — so that every club ices a full roster before anyone is a spare. Once every active seat is filled, the remaining players are loaned out too — spread evenly across the clubs, at random — so every registered player has a club for the pre-season; there is no cap on how many pre-season loans a club carries. A pre-season loan does not change a player's registered position for the draft, counts against no roster limit, and returns to the draft pool when the pre-season ends.","The pre-season does two jobs at once, which is why it exists. It lets every player — new and returning — shake off rust in real games before anything counts; and its table is available to the league office as one basis for the draft order (Rule 2.8) — though a first season draws its order at random, and from the second season on the default is a lottery weighted by the previous regular season. Pre-season games count toward a player’s overall rating but never toward the regular-season standings or the playoff race.","The pre-season also decides who is in the draft. A player who is randomly assigned for the pre-season, and who is not already carried on a club’s full-time roster, must appear in at least three (3) pre-season games to remain draft-eligible (Rule 2.8). Returning players and club management are exempt and may play as much or as little of the pre-season as they wish. Because a player cannot reach three games on his own, club management is obligated to spread pre-season ice time as widely as it can, so that every player who needs the three games has a genuine chance to get them — a Thursday sign-up can still play the final Friday’s three games and make it."],"fullTitle":"Step three — the pre-season"},{"id":"0.5","title":"Step four — the draft","paragraphs":["The draft is held on the Saturday following the sign-up cutoff, conducted live on the site beginning at 9:00 PM Eastern Time. Each club rebuilds its roster by selecting from the pool of players registered by the cutoff, over the number of rounds published for the season (Rule 2.8), in a snake order: the drawn order is followed in the first round, reversed in the second, and alternated thereafter, so that the club selecting last in the first round selects first in the second. The salary of a drafted player is fixed by the round of his selection rather than negotiated — the final round pays the league minimum and each round above it adds one increment — so an earlier selection carries a greater cap hit (Rule 2.8).","The order is drawn at random for each season and bears no relation to the previous season's standings, playoffs or champion; the snake pattern offsets the advantage of an early selection. Each club maintains a private draft board in advance. If a club's clock expires during the draft, the league office selects the highest-ranked available player on that club's own board, so that an absent manager neither forfeits a selection nor receives a player the club did not rank."],"full":["The Saturday after the pre-season's final night is draft night, held live on the site at 7:00 PM Eastern. Clubs rebuild their rosters by selecting from the pool of eligible players in a fixed order that holds for every round — first overall picks first in each of the fourteen rounds — under the order and format the commissioner sets that season (Rule 2.8). What a drafted player earns is set by the round he goes in rather than negotiated — the last round pays the league minimum and every round above it adds a step — so picking earlier costs a club more against its cap (Rule 2.8).","Why a draft at all, and why the order favors last season’s weakest clubs — by default through an NHL-style lottery: drafting redistributes the season's talent so that no club stockpiles a roster across seasons and the weakest teams get first crack at improving. Clubs prepare a private draft board in advance; if a club's clock ever runs out on the night, the league drafts the best available player from that club's own board automatically, so an absent manager never costs his team a pick and never lands a player the club did not rank."]},{"id":"0.6","title":"Step five — placement and puck drop","paragraphs":["Every player registered by the cutoff is draft-eligible (Rule 2.8). Ten (10) minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player who remains without a club is placed on one by the league office, in random order, at the league minimum salary, as depth: a contract for the season in the training camp of a club selected for him, outside the club's active roster and its published composition (Rule 2.8). The active roster remains the players the club drafted and its management. No club selects, and no registered player is left without a club. The basic format has no free-agency period: puck drop is the Wednesday following the draft, and the only signing available to a club during the season is of a player who has been waived, from the conclusion of the draft until the movement deadline (Rule 2.2).","A free-agency period exists to price players whose contracts have ended. Under the basic format every contract ends with its season and every player re-enters the draft, so there is no market to conduct."],"full":["Every player who registered by the deadline and met the three-game pre-season requirement is draft-eligible (Rule 2.8), so the draft covers the on-time pool. Ten minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player still without a club — undrafted, or short of five games — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order, where a club has room in his position group (Rule 2.8). No club chooses, and no rookie goes to free agency. Free agency opens about a day after the draft and runs one full week, and it is for players whose contracts have ended (Rule 2.2): a player in the last season of his deal may talk only to the club that holds it — at any point in that season, and on until the free agency that follows it opens — and to any club once it has (Rule 2.2). In a first season nobody's contract has ended, so free agency is quiet; from the second season on it is where returning players move. The regular season does not start until free agency closes, so every club begins game one with a settled roster."],"fullTitle":"Step five — rookie placement and free agency"},{"id":"0.7","title":"Step six — the regular season","paragraphs":["The regular season begins on the Wednesday following the draft and is played on the shape published in Rule 3.1 — six (6) game-weeks, three nights a week, three games a night, fifty-four (54) games per club. A game-week containing a league-observed holiday is skipped in full and the schedule resumes the following week; the commissioner designates each season's observed holidays in the Control Center. Every active-roster player may be dressed in up to six (6) games a week and a training-camp player in up to three (3) (Rule 5.2); there is no weekly availability quota. The movement deadline falls at the close of the fourth game-week and freezes club-initiated signings, trades and automatic placement (Rule 2.4), so that the final two weeks are contested with settled rosters.","Every game produces a winner. A win in regulation or overtime is worth two (2) points; a loss in overtime is worth one (1); a loss in regulation is worth none. There are no ties and no shootouts (Rule 4.1). Clubs are ranked by total points earned (Rule 8.1), without adjustment for games in hand. A forfeit is scored for points as an ordinary result: two points to the club awarded the win and none to the club that forfeited."],"full":["The regular season starts the Wednesday after free agency closes, on the shape published in Rule 3.1 — the number of weeks, nights and games per club is set season by season and stated there. Game weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped entirely, so no one is asked to play on a holiday; the commissioner chooses each season’s observed holidays in the Control Center. Partway through the season a movement deadline freezes club-initiated signings, trades, and new-player placement (Rule 2.4), so the stretch run is contested with settled rosters.","Every game produces a winner. A win in regulation or overtime is worth two points; a loss in overtime is worth one; a loss in regulation is worth none — there are no ties and no shootouts (Rule 4.1). Clubs are ranked by total points earned (Rule 8.1). Games in hand are not adjusted for: a club that has played more games may sit higher on points alone, and the club beneath it carries the chance to pass it by playing its own. A forfeit is scored for points exactly as an ordinary result, two points to the winner and none to the club that forfeited. Every night, every point pushes toward the one thing the regular season decides: playoff position."]},{"id":"0.8","title":"Step seven — the playoffs","paragraphs":["The playoffs begin in the game-week immediately following the final regular-season week. Six (6) of the league's eight (8) clubs qualify — the top three (3) in each division — and the postseason is played over three (3) rounds in three (3) game-weeks (Chapter 8). In the first round the first seed in each division receives a bye while the second and third seeds meet; in the second round the winner meets the first seed for the division championship; in the third round the two division champions meet in the final. Each round is a best-of-seven series played within a single game-week: two games on Wednesday, two on Thursday and up to three on Friday. Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not change between rounds.","Two rules keep the postseason honest. A player must have appeared in at least the number of regular-season games published for the season as the playoff eligibility floor (Rule 8.3) to be dressed in the playoffs, so that a club's postseason is played by the players who carried its regular season. The floor in force appears on the Seasons page, and Team HQ tracks every player against it through the season. And a playoff appearance cap of four (4) games per player per series applies to everyone, so that no line can carry a club alone (Rule 8.3)."],"full":["The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week, so there is never a dark stretch or an overlap between the two. The top four clubs in each division qualify, an eight-club field, and play a divisional bracket to a champion (Chapter 8). Seeds are fixed when the office certifies the final standings and do not re-seed between rounds.","Two rules keep the postseason honest. Series are decided on depth: the weekly appearance cap applies to each series — a skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games of any single series and a goaltender in at most six (6) (Rule 8.3) — so one line cannot carry a club alone, and three rostered players at every skating position cover all seven games. There is no games-played floor: every rostered player is playoff-eligible."]},{"id":"0.9","title":"Step eight — champion and rollover","paragraphs":["The club that wins the final is declared champion, the result is entered in the permanent record, and the season is marked complete. Registration for the following season opens automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), so that sign-ups for the next season are open while the current season concludes, and the next draft may be held as soon as every club's front office is seated.","Between seasons the league is reset. Every player contract expires with the season for which it was signed; no contract, extension or player right carries into the following season (Rule 2.5). Every player must register again, every registered player enters the next draft, and the order for that draft is drawn at random without reference to the previous season (Rule 2.8). Clubs and their front offices continue; rosters are rebuilt."],"full":["The club that wins the final is crowned champion, the result enters the permanent record, and the season is marked complete. That completion is what starts the clock on the next off-season — the first midnight after that final game — and the whole cycle begins again. One step now happens earlier than the rest: registration for the NEXT season opens automatically the moment the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4), so sign-ups for the following season are already running while the current one plays out.","Between seasons the league rolls over: contracts that reached the end of their term expire and return those players to the pool. Multi-year deals survive — but they no longer place anyone on a roster by themselves. Every player must register for the new season (Rule 2.5); registering returns a contracted player straight to the club, while an unsigned deal sits on the club's cap as dead money with the player unable to play. If the club's owner changed and the player never signed up, the deal is voided and the player suspended for its remaining term. Continuity for the clubs, a fresh draft for the league — and no roster spot handed to anyone who has not shown up for the season. A player whose club re-signed him during the exclusive window (Rule 2.5) is the exception: his new deal comes into force with the new season's cap year and registering returns him to that club; and a club keeps the exclusive right to re-sign a player whose deal just ended until the new season's free agency opens."]}]},{"num":1,"title":"Membership & conduct","sections":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Eligibility and registration","paragraphs":["Membership in the Chel Gaming Hockey League (CGHL) is open to players who complete registration through the league's official channels, link a valid EA account and gamertag, and join the league Discord server. A registration is not accepted without the player's EA ID: it is the name the league's box scores are matched on, and it is shown on the player's public profile and in the player directory so that any member can find him in-game. A player is not eligible to appear in a CGHL game until the league office has confirmed their registration and assigned them to a club or to the free agent pool.","Each player may hold exactly one CGHL identity. Registering, playing under, or lending out a secondary account — including playing under another member's account for any reason — is account misrepresentation and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7. Players are responsible for keeping their gamertag and contact information current with the league office; games played under an unregistered or outdated gamertag may be ruled unofficial.","A registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page at any time until registration for that season ends. Withdrawal removes the player from the sign-up board and the draft pool; a withdrawn player may register again freely while registration remains open. Once a player has been drafted or placed on a club, leaving the season is a roster matter and goes through the league office.","Discord membership is not only an entry requirement — it holds for as long as the sign-up does. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has their sign-up withdrawn automatically: once the player has been out of the server for about a day, the registration is removed from the sign-up board and the draft pool, archived by the league office, and the player is notified on the site. The day of grace exists so a brief absence — a rejoin minutes later, or a Discord hiccup — never costs anyone their sign-up. Rejoining the server restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration and follows the normal draft-eligibility rules. A player already on a club roster or in a training camp is covered as well, under the notice procedure in the paragraph that follows.","When a player who is already on a club roster or in its training camp leaves the league Discord, his club is told before anything is done. A notice goes to the club's room naming the player and the deadline, and the club's management is asked to reach him, because the league cannot: Discord does not deliver a message from the league to a member who has left the server. The player then has twenty four (24) hours from that notice to rejoin. If he rejoins, nothing happens and his club is told so. If he does not, his registration is revoked and archived exactly as it would have been above, he is removed from the club's roster or training camp, and the spot is free for the club to fill. The window runs from the notice, not from the moment the league saw him go, so no player is removed before his club has had a full day to reach him. A player holding a seat in his club's front office (Owner, General Manager or Assistant General Manager) is never removed automatically; the league office is told instead and rules on it directly.","Registration for a season ends when the following season's registration opens, which occurs automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4); from that moment rosters are frozen and new registrations belong to the following season. Each season's sign-up cutoff is 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding that season's draft (Chapter 0, Step two); a player who registers after the cutoff is not excluded but is placed on a club as depth under Rule 2.8."],"full":["Membership in the Chel Gaming Hockey League (CGHL) is open to players who complete registration through the league's official channels, link a valid EA account and gamertag, and join the league Discord server. A player is not eligible to appear in a CGHL game until the league office has confirmed their registration and assigned them to a club or to the free agent pool.","Each player may hold exactly one CGHL identity. Registering, playing under, or lending out a secondary account — including playing under another member's account for any reason — is account misrepresentation and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7. Players are responsible for keeping their gamertag and contact information current with the league office; games played under an unregistered or outdated gamertag may be ruled unofficial.","A registered player may withdraw their own sign-up from the Register page at any time until registration for that season ends. Withdrawal removes the player from the sign-up board and the draft pool; a withdrawn player may register again freely while registration remains open. Once a player has been drafted or placed on a club, leaving the season is a roster matter and goes through the league office.","Discord membership is not only an entry requirement — it holds for as long as the sign-up does. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has their sign-up withdrawn automatically: once the player has been out of the server for about a day, the registration is removed from the sign-up board and the draft pool, archived by the league office, and the player is notified on the site. The day of grace exists so a brief absence — a rejoin minutes later, or a Discord hiccup — never costs anyone their sign-up. Rejoining the server restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration and follows the normal draft-eligibility rules. Players already on a club roster are never touched by this rule — a rostered player who leaves the server is handled by the league office directly.","Registration for a season ends when the following season's registration opens, which happens automatically when the current season's movement deadline passes (Rule 2.4) — from that moment, rosters are frozen and new sign-ups belong to the next season. Each season's draft-eligibility deadline is 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before that season's draft (Chapter 0, Step two); registering after it does not shut a player out, it places them on a club automatically under Rule 2.2."]},{"id":"1.2","title":"Code of conduct","paragraphs":["All members — players, club management, and league staff — are expected to compete hard and act like professionals. Harassment, hate speech, threats, doxxing, and targeted abuse are prohibited in all league spaces, including in-game chat, Discord, streams of league games, and direct messages arising out of league business. The standard applies whether or not the conduct occurs during a scheduled game.","Unsportsmanlike conduct includes, without limitation: deliberate goaltender interference intended to injure or provoke, quitting a game in progress without cause, stat manipulation, throwing games, and public abuse of officials or opponents. The league office may act on conduct violations on its own initiative; it does not require a formal complaint to open a review."]},{"id":"1.3","title":"League communications","paragraphs":["The CGHL Discord server is the league's official channel of record. Schedule releases, rule changes, disciplinary notices, and game codes are delivered through Discord, and every rostered player is responsible for information posted there. A claim of not having seen an official posting is not a defense to a missed obligation under this rulebook.","A club’s management — its Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6) — is the formal point of contact between the club and the league office. Protests, reschedule requests, and roster paperwork must come from the club’s management to be considered filed.","Server-wide pings — @everyone and @here — are reserved to the league office. Only the Commissioner and Staff roles can send them. No department role carries it, so a staffer whose only role is their department — media, for instance — asks the office to post. The permission is re-checked automatically, so a role that gains it by accident loses it again within minutes. Clubs reach their own members with their club role, and anyone who needs the whole league told something should ask the league office to post it. Inside its own club room a club’s management may mention whomever it needs, as often as it needs — the server’s spam filters never block the front office there.","Posting links, images, and file attachments in the Discord requires a league role — that is, an account that has signed in to the website and registered. Anyone may read and chat straight away — and may use Discord's built-in GIF picker, which stays open to everyone. The ability to post any other link or an image is granted upon registration, which completes within seconds; until then, a message containing a link is refused rather than posted. New accounts must also have been in the server ten (10) minutes before they can post at all. These limits exist to stop advertising bots, which join, post a link, and leave; they are not a judgment about any member. Advertising and scam content are blocked automatically and reported to the league office. Server invite links are treated no differently from any other link: a member who has signed up may post one, while an account that has not signed up may not. Club recruitment has its own home — #scouting-links is readable by the whole league, and only a club's Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager may post in it, so what appears there is a club's own invitation and not an open advertising board."]}]},{"num":2,"title":"Rosters & player movement","sections":[{"id":"2.1","title":"Roster composition","paragraphs":["The commissioner shall determine, for each season, the size of the active roster and its composition by position group — the number of forwards, defensemen and goaltenders — and shall publish these figures to the clubs before that season's draft. The composition may be published as a number of complete lines together with a number of additional players of any position, from which the league's roster tools derive the greatest number of forwards, defensemen and goaltenders a club may carry and the total it may not exceed. The figures published for a season are binding for its duration and are enforced by the league's roster tools. The size and composition in force appear on the Seasons page and in Team HQ.","Each club ices from an active roster shaped by position group. A forward may be dressed at center or at either wing, and the forward group may hold centers, left wings and right wings in any mix; a defenseman may be dressed on either side, and the defense group may hold left and right defensemen in any mix; a goaltender may be dressed only in goal, and the goaltending group holds only goaltenders. A club short of left defensemen may carry an additional right defenseman, and a club short of centers an additional wing; only in goal does the declared position bind. A club's Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager are players on its active roster and are counted within the published composition in their own position groups; each holds his roster spot for as long as he holds his seat (Rule 2.6). The active roster must at all times hold enough eligible players to ice a full six-on-six lineup, including a goaltender. A club that falls below that minimum through departures or suspensions shall notify the league office immediately; the vacancy is filled by automatic placement under Rule 2.8 while placement remains open, and by the league office thereafter.","Every active-roster player is assigned a position — center, left wing, right wing, left defense, right defense or goaltender — which governs where he may be dressed under the preceding paragraph. The sole exception is a training-camp player, who may be dressed at any position for as long as he is carried in camp (Rule 5.2).","A club may carry any number of training-camp players in addition to its active roster; the commissioner may publish a camp limit as a season setting, and where none is published camp is unlimited. A training-camp player's salary counts against the club's salary cap exactly as an active player's does (Rule 2.5): camp is carried outside the active roster, not outside the payroll. A training-camp player may be dressed at any position and in no more than three (3) games in a game-week (Rule 5.2). Club management may move a player between the active roster and training camp at any time and without limit, in either direction, provided the club continues to satisfy the published composition; the movement deadline (Rule 2.4) does not restrict these moves, which are internal to the club. Team HQ enforces the composition, the position assignments and any camp limit, and refuses any move that would breach them. A player acquired by trade joins his new club on the active roster.","A player placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8 — a player undrafted, or registered after the cutoff — is carried in the club's training camp as depth: he holds a contract for the season, may be dressed at any position within the camp appearance cap (Rule 5.2), and may be traded or waived as any other player, so that every registered player has a club. Camp is not counted against the published composition. Called up to the active roster, a depth player takes one of its spots like any other player and is subject to the composition and the roster limit; management calls him up only when the active roster has room."],"full":["Each club ices from an active roster of seventeen (17) players shaped by position group: nine (9) forwards — centers, left wings and right wings in any mix, since a forward may be dressed at center or either wing — six (6) defensemen — left or right in any mix, since a defenseman may be dressed on either side — and two (2) goaltenders, the one position locked to its exact role. A club short of left defensemen may carry an extra right defenseman, and a club short of centers an extra wing; only in goal does the listed position bind. The active roster must at all times hold enough eligible players to ice a full 6v6 lineup, including a goaltender. A club that falls below that minimum through departures or suspensions must notify the league office immediately; the vacancy is filled by automatic placement under Rule 2.2 while placement remains open, and by the league office after that.","Every active-roster player is assigned a position — center, left wing, right wing, left defense, right defense, or goaltender — and the assignment governs where he can dress: an active player may be dressed at any position within his group — a forward at center or either wing, a defenseman at either left or right defense — but a skater is never dressed in goal, nor a goaltender out of it. There are two exceptions: a training-camp player may be dressed at any position for as long as he is carried in camp, and in pre-season games only the club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position (Rule 5.2).","A club may carry up to three (3) training-camp players beyond its seventeen active spots. Their salaries count against the club's salary cap exactly as an active player's does (Rule 2.5) — camp is carried beyond the active roster, not beyond the payroll. A training-camp player may be dressed at any position and, in the regular season and the playoffs, in no more than three (3) games in a game week — the cap does not apply in the pre-season (Rule 5.2). Management may move a player between the active roster and training camp freely — as often as it likes, in either direction, at any point in the season — provided the club still satisfies the active-roster shape above — nine forwards, six defensemen, two goaltenders — and carries no more than three players in camp. There is no limit on how many times a player may change squads, and the movement deadline (Rule 2.4) does not restrict these moves, which are internal to the club. Team HQ enforces the roster shape, the position assignments, and the camp limit, and refuses any move that would breach them. A player acquired by trade joins his new club on the active roster. Pre-season loans are the one exception to the roster limit: a randomly assigned pre-season player rides the active roster as a loan, counts against neither the nine-six-two shape nor any roster cap, and returns to the draft pool when the pre-season ends — a club may hold as many pre-season loans as it is assigned, so every registered player has a club for the pre-season (Rule 0.4)."]},{"id":"2.2","title":"Free agency and signings","paragraphs":["The basic format has no free-agency period and no open market for player contracts. Every registered player enters the draft (Rule 2.8); every player remaining after the draft, and every player who registers after the cutoff, is placed on a club by the league office as depth at the league minimum salary (Rule 2.8); and every contract expires with its season, returning every player to the following season's draft pool (Rule 2.5). No club shall offer a contract to an unrostered player, and no player shall negotiate one.","Contact with a rostered player of another club for the purpose of inducing his departure — tampering — is prohibited and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7.","The one signing available to a club is of a player who has been waived (Rule 2.5); a club may waive a player only after he has appeared in three (3) regular-season games in the season (Rule 2.4, minimum service). From the conclusion of the draft until the movement deadline (Rule 2.4), any club with room in the player's position group may sign a waived player at the league minimum salary on a contract running to the end of the season. The club signs him outright: there is no offer, no counter and no acceptance — in this format clubs move players and players are not asked. The signing takes effect when it is made, places the player on the club's official roster immediately, and is posted to the transaction log automatically; the league office confirms nothing and has no part in the move. A player shall not dress for a club before he has been signed by it, and any game in which an unsigned player dresses is subject to forfeiture under Chapter 3.","Automatic placement does not apply to a suspended or banned player, who is placed nowhere until reinstated. The basic format recognizes no classes of player rights: no club holds a player's rights once his season ends, and no player is restricted. Should the league designate the full format for a later season, the rights provisions of Appendix A apply from that season, and the league office shall publish, before that season's registration opens, the manner in which a returning player enters it."],"full":["Unrostered registered players are held in the league free agent pool. A club signs a free agent by offering him terms; the signing takes effect the moment the player accepts, and he joins the club’s official roster there and then. The league office confirms nothing and has no say in the move. A player may not dress for a club before he has accepted its offer, and any game in which an unsigned player dresses is subject to forfeiture under Chapter 3.","Contact with rostered players of other clubs for the purpose of inducing a departure — tampering — is prohibited and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7.","A registered player who is neither drafted nor signed is held in the free agent pool, and no registered member is left without a place to play while placement is open. The league office automatically places an unrostered registered player — including anyone who registers after the draft-eligibility deadline or joins mid-season — onto a club that sits below a full active roster, at the league minimum salary. Automatic placement is a league-office action to fill a vacancy, not a club-initiated signing — but it shares the movement deadline’s cutoff: random placement of new players closes at the deadline (Rule 2.4), and a player who registers after it waits for the next season’s draft pool.","Automatic placement never applies to a player under contract: a contracted player who registers is returned to the club that holds the deal (Rule 2.5), never routed to the free agent pool, the draft, or a random assignment. Nor does it apply to a suspended or banned player, who is not placed anywhere until reinstated.","Available players fall into two classes of rights, on the model the NHL uses. A player who has never completed a season on a club's roster is an UNRESTRICTED free agent: he is free to sign with any club that wants him. Once a player has served a club through a season, his rights stay with that club when his contract ends, and he is a RESTRICTED free agent until he has accrued four (4) off-seasons of service, counted one per season completed on a roster. How a former club exercises those rights when another club makes him an offer is set by the league office and published before the first restricted class appears, after Season 1. From the fourth accrued off-season onward he is unrestricted for good. Because the league has no history in its first season, every registered player begins unrestricted; the first restricted free agents appear in the off-season that follows a player's first year on a roster. The number of off-seasons is a league-office setting, and the league publishes it before each signing window opens. A player's current class is shown on his profile and carried by his Discord role. Free agency is for players whose contracts have ended. It is not for rookies: an undrafted first-year is placed on a club by the league office ten minutes after the draft (Rule 2.8) and is never offered a contract on the open market. A player whose contract is in its final season may negotiate only with the club that holds it — either side may open the conversation — at any point in that season, from the day its free agency opens until the free-agency period that FOLLOWS his contract's final season opens — approaching, or being approached by, any other club in that window is tampering under the paragraph above — and may negotiate with any club once that free agency has opened. The club's exclusive right survives the contract's expiry at the season rollover and lapses only when that free agency opens. It neither adds to nor takes from the club's rights over him as a restricted free agent. How a returning player whose deal has ended enters the next season — through that free agency, or through the entry draft — is set by the league office and published before that season's registration opens; the exclusive window and restricted-free-agent rights described here apply from the end of Season 1 onward.","A player whose contract has ended enters open free agency. Clubs send that player contract offers at a salary and term of their choosing, within the cap and contract limits of Rule 2.5. The player may accept, decline, or counter with his own number, and the club may then accept his counter, revise again, or walk away. A signing takes effect the instant the player accepts — he is on the roster immediately, accepting one offer withdraws every other offer he holds, and the move is posted to the transaction log automatically. Players are expected in good faith to take the strongest offer on the table. The league does not forbid a player from signing a lower offer — to play with friends, for instance — but it trusts members not to abuse that latitude to defeat a fair market."]},{"id":"2.3","title":"Trades","paragraphs":["Clubs may trade rostered players by mutual agreement of both clubs' management, provided every player in the trade has appeared in three (3) regular-season games in the season (Rule 2.4, minimum service). Draft picks are not tradeable assets in the basic format: a trade consists of players for players, and an offer that includes a draft pick is refused at the point of entry. A trade takes effect when the receiving club accepts it in the Trade Hub; it is posted to the official transaction log automatically, and a player acquired by trade is eligible for his new club from that club's next scheduled game.","The league office may void any trade it determines was made in bad faith, including a trade intended to circumvent roster limits, to manipulate playoff seeding, or to hold players on behalf of another club.","The league office takes no part in a trade as it is made: the agreement of the two clubs suffices, and the salary-cap and roster checks are applied automatically at acceptance. The transactions department may thereafter review any completed trade and reverse it where, in the department's judgment, it is not a bona fide hockey transaction — a lopsided transfer between allied clubs, or a transaction made to defeat the cap. A reversal returns every player to the club that held him, is posted to the transaction log, and is communicated to both clubs with the department's reasons. A trade may not be reversed once a player included in it has been moved again; the department acts promptly or not at all."],"full":["Clubs may trade rostered players and draft picks by mutual agreement of both clubs’ management. A trade takes effect the moment the second club accepts it in the Trade Hub; it is posted to the official transaction log automatically, and players acquired by trade are eligible for their new club beginning with that club’s next scheduled game.","The league office may void any trade it determines was made in bad faith, including trades intended to circumvent roster limits, manipulate playoff seeding, or park players on behalf of another club.","The league has no say in a trade as it is made: two clubs agreeing is enough, and the salary-cap and roster checks run automatically as the trade is accepted. Afterwards the transactions department may review any completed trade and send it back where, in its judgment, it is not a natural hockey deal — a lopsided giveaway between allied clubs, or a move made to defeat the cap. Reversing a trade returns every player and pick to the club that held it, is posted to the league transaction log, and both clubs are told the reason in the department's own words. A trade cannot be reversed once a player or pick in it has moved on again; the department acts promptly or not at all."]},{"id":"2.4","title":"Movement deadline","paragraphs":["All player movement — the signing of waived players, trades and releases — closes at midnight Eastern Time at the end of the Friday of the fourth (4th) game-week, being the league-posted deadline week announced before the season. After the deadline, rosters are frozen through the end of the playoffs, except that the league office may authorize an emergency goaltender signing for a club unable to ice a goaltender through no fault of its own.","The movement deadline restricts club-initiated signings, trades and releases, and it is also the point at which automatic placement closes: the league office places no newly registered player after the deadline, so that late-season rosters reflect only what clubs did before it. Movement between a club's own active roster and its training camp is internal to the club — no player changes hands — and is not restricted by the deadline (Rule 2.1). Registration for the following season opens when the deadline passes (Rule 1.1).","Minimum service. A club may not waive or trade a player who has appeared in fewer than three (3) regular-season games in the current season, whether he is on its active roster or in its training camp. Appearances are counted from the official record (Chapter 6) across every club the player was rostered with in the season. The rule exists so that a club plays the players it drafted or was assigned before it moves them, and so that no club acquires a player whose ability neither club has yet seen. The league's roster tools and Trade Hub refuse a waiver or an offer involving such a player and show the games he still needs. A player placed by the league office is subject to it like any other; management, who cannot be waived or traded, is not affected."],"full":["All player movement — signings, trades, and releases — closes at midnight at the end of the Friday of the league-posted deadline week, which is announced before the season. After the deadline, rosters are frozen through the end of the playoffs, except that the league office may authorize an emergency goaltender signing for a club unable to ice a goaltender through no fault of its own, and except a contract extension under Rule 2.5, which changes no roster this season.","The movement deadline restricts club-initiated signings, trades, and releases — and it is also the moment automatic placement closes: the league office does not randomly place newly registered players after the deadline, so late-season rosters are shaped only by what clubs did before it. A contracted player who registers is returned to his own club at any time under Rule 2.5; that is a contract right, not a placement, and no deadline touches it. Movement between a club's own active roster and its training camp is internal to the club — no player changes hands — and the deadline does not restrict it (Rule 2.1)."]},{"id":"2.5","title":"Salary cap and contracts","paragraphs":["Every club operates under a hard salary cap, set by the commissioner for each season and published on every club's cap sheet — {{CAP}} for the current season. A club shall not ice a lineup, complete a trade or claim a waived player if the move would place its payroll above the cap. A training-camp player's salary forms part of that payroll: camp is carried outside the active roster but not outside the cap. A club that carries a player in camp bears his full salary against the cap from the moment he is placed there, whether or not he dresses, and recalling him to the active roster changes nothing about his cost. A depth placement's salary counts in the same manner. Compliance is verified automatically at every roster move, and the league office may void any transaction that breaches the cap.","Every player holds a contract with a cap hit and a term of one (1) season — the season in which it is signed. A drafted player's contract carries the salary fixed for his round by Rule 2.8; a depth placement's contract, and a waived player's on re-signing, carries the league minimum salary. The league minimum salary is $750,000, and every salary in the league is a multiple of $250,000 above it — $750,000, $1,000,000, $1,250,000 and so on. That increment governs every salary in the league: the draft scale of Rule 2.8 is fixed on it, and the fixed management values of Rule 2.6 lie on the same lattice. A figure off the lattice is refused where it is entered rather than rounded.","The basic format provides for no contract extension, no re-signing and no retained player rights. Every contract expires when its season ends, the player returns to the pool, and he enters the following season's draft on the same footing as every other registered player (Rule 2.8). A club may not offer a player terms for a future season, and nothing a club does in a season confers any claim on a player for the next. The cap year is the season.","Waiving a player removes his cap hit from the club immediately and opens a twenty-four (24) hour waiver period. A claiming club assumes the player at his pre-waiver salary, reinstated as it stood; a player who clears waivers unclaimed may be signed by any club with room in his position group, at the league minimum salary, until the movement deadline (Rule 2.2). A player returning from a league suspension is reinstated at his pre-suspension salary.","Cap space, contract values and trade-block status are confidential club information, visible only to the club's management group and the league office, and are subject to the confidentiality standard of Rule 2.3.","A contract does not substitute for registration. Every player must register for every season; registration is what enters a player in the draft pool (Rule 2.8). Because no contract outlives its season, a player who does not register sits the season out; nothing is owed to him and nothing is charged against any club's cap."],"full":["Every club operates under a hard salary cap, set by the commissioner for each season and published on every club’s cap sheet — {{CAP}} for the current season. A club may not ice a lineup, complete a trade, or claim a waived player if the move would push its active payroll above the cap. A training-camp player's salary is part of that payroll: camp sits outside the seventeen active spots, but not outside the cap. A club that carries a player in camp pays his full salary against the cap from the moment he is placed there, whether or not he ever dresses, and calling him up to the active roster changes nothing about what he costs. Compliance is checked automatically at every roster move, and the league office may void any transaction that breaches it.","Every player carries a contract with a cap hit and a term of one to three seasons; a drafted player's first contract, and a contract written by automatic placement, runs exactly one season at the salary Rule 2.8 fixes; a new deal may follow it only through the extension window below or through free agency. The league minimum salary is $750,000, and every salary in the league is a multiple of $250,000 — the minimum, then $1,000,000, $1,250,000, $1,500,000 and so on upward. That single step governs every salary in the league, whether it is negotiated or set by rule: open free agency under Rule 2.2, every counter and revision inside a negotiation, and the draft scale of Rule 2.8, which is fixed rather than agreed. The fixed management values of Rule 2.6 sit on the same lattice. A figure off that lattice is refused where it is entered rather than quietly rounded, so the number a club offers is always the number the player is asked to accept. A contract that reaches the end of its term returns the player to free agency under Rule 2.2 unless he has signed an extension (below) — and until that free agency opens, the club that held the deal alone may re-sign him.","The cap year runs from one free-agency opening to the next. A contract's money counts only against the seasons it covers; when its final season ends the deal is closed at the season rollover, but the club's exclusive right to re-sign the player survives until the free agency that follows that season opens — until then he is neither a free agent nor anyone else's to approach. A club re-signs its own player through an extension: at any point in the final season of his deal — from the day that season's free agency opens — the club may offer him a new contract from Team HQ, or he may open with his own number from his dashboard, and the two negotiate exactly as in free agency — offer, counter, revise, accept or walk away — at a salary on the $250,000 lattice and a term of one to three seasons. An extension accepted during the final season is a signed deal for the following season, and comes into force with that season's cap year: nothing about the current season changes, and it is checked against the following season's cap — counting every deal already signed for that season and the three front-office seats at their fixed values — rather than this season's, because the space a club will have then is what it will have once its expiring deals have turned over. The site refuses any signing, now or for a future season, that would not fit inside the cap it is checked against; there is no exception. A player re-signed after the rollover, before that season's free agency opens, is signed for the season now under way: the deal takes effect the moment he accepts, is checked against that season's cap, and seats him on the club once he is registered. A signed extension travels with the player if he is traded and is voided if he is waived. A player may hold one signed extension at a time, and while he holds one no club may offer him another. No club other than the one that holds his current deal may offer him anything until that deal has ended and free agency has opened (Rule 2.2). Team HQ shows every club its committed money and cap space for the current season and the three that follow, from the same arithmetic the cap checks use, so a club plans against exactly what will be enforced.","Waiving a player clears his cap hit immediately and opens a 24-hour waiver window. A claiming club takes on the player at his pre-waiver salary, reinstated exactly as it stood; a player who clears unclaimed becomes a free agent. A player returning from a league suspension is reinstated at his pre-suspension salary and remaining term.","Cap space, contract values, and trade-block status are confidential team information. They are visible only to a club's management group and the league office, and are covered by the confidentiality standard in Rule 2.3.","A contract does not replace registration. Every player must register for every season — including every season of a multi-year deal. Registering while under contract returns the player directly to the club that holds the deal, at the contracted salary: no draft, no free agency, no random assignment. The sign-up requirement in Chapter 0 applies to everyone, veteran and rookie, signed and unsigned alike.","If a player under contract fails to register and the club's ownership is unchanged, the deal becomes dead money: the full salary continues to count against the club's salary cap, and the player cannot play — no roster spot exists, and the player may not be dressed, drafted, signed, or placed anywhere. The hold lasts until the player registers, which reactivates the deal at no net cap change, or until the contract's term ends.","If the club changes hands during the off-season — a different owner takes the seat between one season and the next — and the contracted player is still unregistered past the sign-up deadline, the contract is voided: the salary comes off the books entirely, and the player is suspended through what would have been the deal's final season. A club that changes owners is never saddled with an unsigned player's salary, and a player who walks away sits out exactly the seasons the contract covered. (A club that simply sits without an owner for a stretch has not changed hands — its unsigned deals stay as dead money until the player registers, nothing is voided.) These suspensions are recorded in the league's discipline log and lift automatically when their final season completes."]},{"id":"2.6","title":"Team management structure","paragraphs":["Each club is led by an Owner, who is appointed by the league office through the application process, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager. The front-office seats are the Owner’s alone: the Owner nominates the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager — a nomination goes to the league office’s reviewers, who approve or deny it — and the Owner may remove a sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager at any time, without a league-office step; the removal takes effect at once, the seat falls vacant, and the league office is told. A General Manager or Assistant General Manager nominates and removes nobody. Each seat is held by one person and one person only, and one person holds at most one club seat league-wide. An appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused: the sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager steps down, or is removed by the Owner or the league office, before a successor can be approved, so no manager is displaced by an appointment without being told. The league office retains its power to seat and unseat management directly under this chapter, including removal under the standards set out below. These roles form the club’s management group and are the only accounts with access to the club’s Team HQ and its confidential team tools — the availability grid, the lineup builder, the roster cap sheet, the free-agent board, the draft board and the Trade Hub. Every member of the management group — Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager alike — has the same Team HQ, subject only to the access the Owner sets under the next paragraph. Every roster move that touches the club — a signing, waiver or release, each step of a trade, a contract offer and its answer, a pre-season loan, a squad move or a trade-block flag — is delivered to the whole management group as a site notification and as a post in the club’s private Discord room the moment it is recorded, alongside the public transaction log; a placement made in bulk by the league office arrives as one summary naming the players. Every club must hold its Owner and General Manager seats before the entry draft begins; the Assistant General Manager's seat may be filled later (Rule 2.8).","The Owner sets what each manager may do, from Team HQ → Management, for every front-office page — the roster, the lineup builder, the schedule’s server picks, the game stats desk, the Trade Hub, the free-agent board and the draft. For each page the General Manager’s access and the Assistant General Manager’s access is one of three things: full, in which case the manager acts freely; subject to the Owner’s approval, in which case the manager sees the page but every move made on it — a waiver, a contract or extension offer, a trade proposed, accepted or declined, a lineup or line, a server pick, a draft pick or a draft-board change — is queued and takes effect only when the Owner approves it; or withheld, in which case the page is hidden from that seat and its moves are refused. The default for every seat and page is full access, with one exception: the Management page — the front office, this permissions table and the approval queue — is the Owner’s alone and is withheld from the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager unless the Owner opens it to them. A queued move runs through the league’s ordinary checks at the moment it is approved, and a move that can no longer be made by then — a lineup past its lock, an offer already withdrawn, a pick already used — fails rather than half-applies, and both the Owner and the manager are told why. What the Owner reads and approves is a description the league builds from the move itself — the player, the terms, the clubs, the lineup — never text the manager wrote, so what is approved is what runs. A manager may withdraw a queued move before the Owner decides it, and a newer request for the same thing from the same manager replaces the older one still waiting. The Owner’s own moves never need approval, the Owner cannot limit the Owner’s own seat, and none of this limits the league office. The game stats desk and this Management page carry no roster or cap moves, so they are either open to a seat or withheld — there is nothing on them to approve.","Management contracts carry fixed cap values: the Owner and the General Manager each count $0 against the cap, and the Assistant General Manager (and any additional tertiary management contract) counts a flat $2,000,000. A manager is a player on his club's active roster and, under the basic format, holds one of the club's active-roster spots in his own position group within the published composition (Rule 2.1). A player is protected while he holds a management role: he may not be waived, and he may not be included in a trade, until the role is vacated.","Owner and staff applications with no open seat may be placed on a waitlist by the league office. When a seat opens — an owner resigns or is not renewed, or a staff member steps down — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in the order applications were waitlisted; eligibility is re-checked at appointment against Rule 2.7, so a waitlisted applicant who has since taken a conflicting seat is passed over, not appointed. The appointee and the commissioners are notified the moment it happens, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review.","Managers are held to the activity and on-ice standards the commissioner sets each season — minimum weekly availability and, where applicable, baseline performance thresholds by position. A manager who falls short of those standards may be reviewed and, if warranted, removed and replaced under this chapter.","Trade discussions, offers, and block listings are restricted to the management groups involved and the league office. Carrying that information outside the management group — to players, to rival clubs, or to the public — is a conduct violation under Rule 1.2 and Rule 2.3."],"full":["Each club is led by an Owner, who is appointed by the league office through the application process, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager. The front-office seats are the Owner’s alone: the Owner nominates the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager — a nomination goes to the league office’s reviewers, who approve or deny it — and the Owner may remove a sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager at any time, without a league-office step; the removal takes effect at once, the seat falls vacant, and the league office is told. A General Manager or Assistant General Manager nominates and removes nobody. Each seat is held by one person and one person only, and one person holds at most one club seat league-wide. An appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused: the sitting General Manager or Assistant General Manager steps down, or is removed by the Owner or the league office, before a successor can be approved, so no manager is displaced by an appointment without being told. The league office retains its power to seat and unseat management directly under this chapter, including removal under the standards set out below. These roles form the club’s management group and are the only accounts with access to the club’s Team HQ and its confidential team tools — the availability grid, the lineup builder, the roster cap sheet, the free-agent board, the draft board and the Trade Hub. Every member of the management group — Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager alike — has the same Team HQ, subject only to the access the Owner sets under the next paragraph. Every roster move that touches the club — a signing, waiver or release, each step of a trade, a contract offer and its answer, a pre-season loan, a squad move or a trade-block flag — is delivered to the whole management group as a site notification and as a post in the club’s private Discord room the moment it is recorded, alongside the public transaction log; a placement made in bulk by the league office arrives as one summary naming the players. Every club must hold all three seats before the entry draft begins (Rule 2.8).","The Owner sets what each manager may do, from Team HQ → Management, for every front-office page — the roster, the lineup builder, the schedule’s server picks, the game stats desk, the Trade Hub, the free-agent board and the draft. For each page the General Manager’s access and the Assistant General Manager’s access is one of three things: full, in which case the manager acts freely; subject to the Owner’s approval, in which case the manager sees the page but every move made on it — a waiver, a contract or extension offer, a trade proposed, accepted or declined, a lineup or line, a server pick, a draft pick or a draft-board change — is queued and takes effect only when the Owner approves it; or withheld, in which case the page is hidden from that seat and its moves are refused. The default for every seat and page is full access, with one exception: the Management page — the front office, this permissions table and the approval queue — is the Owner’s alone and is withheld from the General Manager and the Assistant General Manager unless the Owner opens it to them. A queued move runs through the league’s ordinary checks at the moment it is approved, and a move that can no longer be made by then — a lineup past its lock, an offer already withdrawn, a pick already used — fails rather than half-applies, and both the Owner and the manager are told why. What the Owner reads and approves is a description the league builds from the move itself — the player, the terms, the clubs, the lineup — never text the manager wrote, so what is approved is what runs. A manager may withdraw a queued move before the Owner decides it, and a newer request for the same thing from the same manager replaces the older one still waiting. The Owner’s own moves never need approval, the Owner cannot limit the Owner’s own seat, and none of this limits the league office. The game stats desk and this Management page carry no roster or cap moves, so they are either open to a seat or withheld — there is nothing on them to approve.","Management contracts carry fixed cap values: the Owner counts $0 against the cap, and the General Manager and Assistant General Manager (and any additional tertiary management contract) each count a flat $3,000,000. A player is protected while he holds a management role — he may not be waived, and he may not be included in a trade, until the role is vacated.","Owner and staff applications with no open seat may be placed on a waitlist by the league office. When a seat opens — an owner resigns or is not renewed, or a staff member steps down — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in the order applications were waitlisted; eligibility is re-checked at appointment against Rule 2.7, so a waitlisted applicant who has since taken a conflicting seat is passed over, not appointed. The appointee and the commissioners are notified the moment it happens, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review.","Managers are held to the activity and on-ice standards the commissioner sets each season — minimum weekly availability and, where applicable, baseline performance thresholds by position. A manager who falls short of those standards may be reviewed and, if warranted, removed and replaced under this chapter.","Trade discussions, offers, and block listings are restricted to the management groups involved and the league office. Carrying that information outside the management group — to players, to rival clubs, or to the public — is a conduct violation under Rule 1.2 and Rule 2.3."]},{"id":"2.7","title":"League staff and management separation","paragraphs":["The league office and a club's front office may not be held at once, with the single exception set out below. A commissioner or staff member may not own or manage a club — no Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager role — and, the other way round, anyone holding a club's Owner, GM, or AGM seat may not sit on the staff. Within the office, a commissioner may serve as staff: commissioners and staff work disputes, discipline, and applications together. What the separation forbids is holding a league-office seat and a club's front-office seat at the same time, so decisions about teams and staff are made without a stake in the outcome.","Media is a department apart, and an exclusive one. A staff member on Media holds Media and no other department, and holds no ballot: not on a staff vote of any kind, not on a rule change, and not on a season award. The department reports on the league rather than deciding for it. In exchange, Media is the one department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM, or Assistant GM seat — and the two halves of that bargain are what make each other safe, because a staff member who decides nothing cannot decide anything in favor of the club they run. A staff member who wants a second department, or a vote, gives up both the Media seat and, if they hold one, the club seat. None of this binds a commissioner, whose office carries every department and every vote by definition.","Staff and commissioners may still play. Holding an office does not bar a member from being a rostered player on a club; it bars only the management roles that would put them on both sides of a league decision.","A former staff member must sit out one full season before taking a management role, and a former commissioner must sit out two, so that the authority of the office does not follow a member straight onto a club's bench. The league office may grant a narrow exception only where a club would otherwise fold, and only as a documented last resort."]},{"id":"2.8","title":"The entry draft","paragraphs":["Rosters are rebuilt each season through an entry draft held on the Saturday following the sign-up cutoff (Chapter 0). The commissioner shall determine the number of rounds for each season's draft and shall publish it to the clubs, together with the roster composition of Rule 2.1 and the playoff eligibility floor of Rule 8.3, before the draft; the number published for a season is binding for its duration, and the draft board is built to it. Clubs select in a snake order — the drawn order is followed in the first round, reversed in the second and alternated thereafter — with selections made live on the site against a per-selection clock. The season's calendar around the draft is set out in Chapter 0; this rule states the binding requirements.","No club shall draft without its leadership seated. Every club must hold an Owner and a General Manager (Rule 2.6) before the draft may begin; the Assistant General Manager's seat may be open and filled afterward. The league office seats any open Owner or General Manager post first; the draft does not start while one of those seats is vacant, and the draft room identifies any club still short before the night.","The draft order is drawn at random by the commissioner before the draft and published to the clubs before it begins. Each season's order is drawn afresh and bears no relation to the previous season's standings, playoffs or champion; an expansion club enters the draw on equal terms with every other club. The commissioner may substitute a manual order only where the league office has ruled that one is required.","Every player who registers by the sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft (Chapter 0, Step two) — enters the draft pool. There is no appearance requirement and no exemption: registration by the cutoff is the sole test. A registration filed after the cutoff does not enter the pool; that player is placed on a club as depth under the placement paragraph below, until placement closes at the movement deadline.","A drafted player is paid according to the round of his selection and not his position within it. The final round pays the league minimum salary, and each round above it adds one increment of $250,000 (Rule 2.5): in a draft of N rounds, round N pays $750,000 and round one pays $750,000 plus $250,000 multiplied by (N − 1), so that the scale follows the number of rounds published for the season and the final round always pays the minimum. The full scale for the season is published with the round count, before the draft, and is displayed in the draft room. Every selection in a round carries an identical cap hit — the first overall selection and the last selection of the round cost a club the same — and a selection deferred under the following paragraphs and used later carries the cap hit of its own round. Salary is fixed by this scale at the moment the selection is made. A player placed on a club under paragraph 8 for a selection it never used is not a selection and is paid the league minimum.","Each club shall maintain a private draft board — a ranked list of the players it wishes to select, visible only to that club's management and the league office. The board is the club's own tool: on the clock, the club selects from it in one action. If a club's clock expires, the selection is skipped and no player is selected on the club's behalf; the skipped selection remains the club's to use before the draft is concluded (paragraph 7), and a selection still unused when the draft is concluded is replaced under paragraph 8.","Draft picks are not club assets under the basic format and may not be traded (Rule 2.3). A club uses its own selection in every round or defers it; a deferred selection is not forfeited and remains the club's to use at any time before the draft is concluded.","The commissioner conducts the draft from the Control Center and may pause and resume it, reset the selection clock, defer a selection, reverse a completed selection, make a selection on a club's behalf, or conclude the draft. Concluding the draft releases any unused selections and begins a ten (10) minute period, after which the league office places players in two passes. First, a club with one or more selections it never used (a clock that expired, a deferral never taken, a selection released at conclusion) receives one registered player per such selection, chosen at random from the players still without a club who fit its published composition, onto its active roster at the league minimum salary, so that its roster is filled. Then every registered player still without a club is placed on one by the league office, in random order, at the league minimum salary, as depth in the training camp of the club then carrying the fewest players (Rule 2.1), on a contract for the season (Rule 2.5). No club selects, and no registered player is left without a club. The same placement seats every late registrant until the movement deadline closes placement (Rule 2.4). The ten-minute period exists so that the league office may still reverse a selection."],"full":["Rosters are rebuilt each season through an entry draft held after the pre-season (Chapter 0). Clubs select in the same order in every round — the order never reverses — over fourteen (14) rounds, with picks made live on the site against a per-pick clock. The overall narrative of the season's calendar around the draft is set out in Chapter 0; this rule states the binding requirements.","No club drafts without a full front office. Every club must hold an Owner, a General Manager and an Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6) before the draft can begin. The league office seats any open post first; the draft will not start while a seat is empty, and the draft room names any club still short so nobody discovers it on the night.","The draft order is set by the commissioner in one of five ways: by an NHL-style draft lottery drawn from the previous season — clubs that missed the playoffs draw weighted odds for the top two selections, with the weakest club holding the most tickets, the rest of the non-playoff group follows in reverse standings, and playoff clubs pick after them in reverse order of finish, the champion last; by reverse pre-season standings, so the weakest club picks first; by a weighted lottery over the pre-season table, in which every club can win the first pick but the weakest hold the most tickets; by a pure random shuffle; or by a manual order the commissioner arranges. A first season, having no previous season to draw on, uses a pure random shuffle in place of the lottery. A club with no previous-season record of its own — an expansion club — enters the lottery as if it had finished last. Whichever method is used, the drawn order is published to the clubs before the draft begins.","Every player who registers by the draft-eligibility deadline enters the draft pool, provided he meets the pre-season appearance requirement. A player who is randomly assigned for the pre-season, and who is not already carried on a club's full-time roster, must appear in at least three (3) pre-season games to remain draft-eligible. Returning players and club management are exempt from the requirement and may play the pre-season or sit it out as they choose. Club management is obligated to distribute pre-season appearances as widely as it can, so that every player who needs three games has a real opportunity to play them; a club that leaves eligible players short without cause is reviewed by the league office. The deadline is 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft (Chapter 0.3); a registration filed after it is never in the pool, however many pre-season games its owner plays. A player who registers after the deadline is not lost: he is placed on a club automatically under Rule 2.2, up until random placement closes at the movement deadline.","A drafted player is paid by the round in which he is selected rather than by his position inside it. The final round of the draft pays the league minimum, and each round above it adds one $250,000 step (Rule 2.5) — so the scale is set by the number of rounds the commissioner calls that season, and the last round always pays the minimum however many there are. In this season's fourteen-round draft that is round one $4,000,000, round two $3,750,000, round three $3,500,000, round four $3,250,000, round five $3,000,000, round six $2,750,000, round seven $2,500,000, round eight $2,250,000, round nine $2,000,000, round ten $1,750,000, round eleven $1,500,000, round twelve $1,250,000, round thirteen $1,000,000 and round fourteen $750,000, for a full class of $33,250,000 — which, with the three management contracts of Rule 2.6, brings a club that uses every pick to within $750,000 of the cap before free agency opens. Every pick in a round carries an identical cap hit — first overall and tenth overall cost a club the same. A club that trades within a round moves no money; a club that trades across rounds moves one step per round, and a pick that is skipped and used later still costs its own round. Salary is fixed by this scale at the moment the pick is made, whether the club picks for itself or its clock expires and the league picks from its board.","Each club keeps a private draft board — a ranked list of the players it wants, visible only to that club's management and the league office. If a club's clock expires on the night, the league selects the best available player from that club's own board, and only if the board is exhausted does it fall to the best available eligible player overall. A club is never auto-assigned a player it did not rank while its board still holds an available name.","Draft picks are club assets and may be traded under the ordinary trade rules of Rule 2.3, within the cap of Rule 2.5 — before the draft and during it, exactly as on a real draft floor. A traded pick belongs to its new club the moment the trade is accepted, including a pick currently on the clock, whose timer continues unchanged. A pick that has already been used cannot be traded, and an offer containing one is refused as stale. Trades made during the draft are announced on the transaction wire like any other.","The commissioner runs the draft from the Control Center and may pause and resume it, reset the pick clock, skip a pick, reverse a completed pick, make a pick on a club's behalf, or conclude the draft. A skipped pick is not forfeited — it remains the club's to use at any time before the draft ends. Concluding the draft releases any unused picks and begins a ten-minute countdown, after which every registered player still without a club — undrafted, or short of the three-game requirement — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order, seated where a club has room in his position group (Rule 2.1). No club chooses. The placement is a one-season contract (Rule 2.5). The ten minutes exist so the office can still reverse a pick; anyone the office cannot seat, because every club with room at his position is at its cap or roster limit, is reported to the commissioners rather than left in silence."]},{"id":"2.9","title":"Position changes","paragraphs":["A member's position is declared at registration and governs his placement: clubs draft against positional need, and the league office places undrafted and late-registering players by declared position. A member who wishes to change his declared position shall file a position-change request with the league office from the league office page. Staff decide the request; no member decides his own. This rule governs the position at which a player is registered; Rule 2.1 governs where a rostered player may be dressed on a given night.","Position changes close at the sign-up cutoff — 11:59 PM Eastern Time on the Thursday preceding the draft. After the cutoff a member's declared position is fixed for the season: no request may be filed and none may be approved. A request already pending may be declined and closed so that none remains open. The deadline follows the published calendar rather than a fixed date — if the draft is re-spaced, the deadline moves with it — and the commissioner retains a narrow authority to correct a position after the cutoff where the error was the league office's own.","The cutoff is placed there because the draft order and every club's draft board are prepared against the positions with which the pool is published, and the league office places the undrafted by the same positions; closing at the cutoff ensures that the pool the clubs draft from is the pool they prepared against."],"full":["A member's position is chosen when he registers and governs where he is placed: the pre-season assigns players across clubs by position, and clubs draft and sign against positional need. A member who wants a different position files a position-change request with the league office from the league office page. Staff decide it, and no member decides his own request. This rule governs the position a player is registered AT; Rule 2.1 separately governs where a rostered player may be dressed on a given night.","Position changes close at 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game. After that a member's position is set for the season: no request may be filed, and none may be approved. A request already sitting in the queue may still be declined and closed, so nothing is left hanging. The deadline follows the published schedule rather than a fixed calendar date — if the pre-season is re-spaced, the deadline moves with it — and the commissioner retains a narrow power to correct a position after it where the league office itself made the error.","Why it closes there: a position that changes after the pre-season has started invalidates both of the things built on it. Clubs are assigned their pre-season rosters by position, and the draft order and every club's board are prepared against the positions the pool is published with. Closing on the Tuesday leaves the day before the opener for the office to settle the list and for clubs to see the pool they will actually be playing and drafting from."]}]},{"num":3,"title":"Scheduling, forfeits & reschedules","sections":[{"id":"3.1","title":"The official schedule","paragraphs":["The CGHL regular season runs six (6) game-weeks — three nights a week, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, three games a night, for 54 games per club — with matchups and puck-drop times published on the official league schedule. A game-week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule steps over that week and runs one week later, so the game count above always holds. The schedule as posted by the league office is binding on all clubs. Puck-drop times are stated in Eastern Time unless otherwise noted, and clubs are responsible for converting to their local time. Game-weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped in full; the commissioner sets each season’s observed holidays, and the schedule as generated already reflects them. The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week — if an observed holiday pushes the season later, the playoff date moves with it, so the regular season can never run into its own postseason.","Clubs are expected to be lobby-ready at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lobby-ready means a full eligible lineup is online, in the league Discord, and prepared to enter the private game upon code release under Rule 4.2."],"full":["The CGHL regular season runs eight (8) game-weeks — three nights a week, Wednesday, Thursday and Friday, three games a night, for 72 games per club — with matchups and puck-drop times published on the official league schedule. A game-week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule steps over that week and runs one week later, so the game count above always holds. The schedule as posted by the league office is binding on all clubs. Puck-drop times are stated in Eastern Time unless otherwise noted, and clubs are responsible for converting to their local time. Game-weeks holding a league-observed holiday are skipped in full; the commissioner sets each season’s observed holidays, and the schedule as generated already reflects them. The playoffs open the game week immediately after the final regular-season week — if an observed holiday pushes the season later, the playoff date moves with it, so the regular season can never run into its own postseason.","Clubs are expected to be lobby-ready at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lobby-ready means a full eligible lineup is online, in the league Discord, and prepared to enter the private game upon code release under Rule 4.2."]},{"id":"3.2","title":"Late starts, the grace period and forfeits","paragraphs":["Games begin with a search at the scheduled puck-drop time. Lateness is measured from that time and is answered with penalties served at the start of the game once it begins: at five (5) minutes past game time the late club takes one (1) penalty; at eight (8) minutes past, two (2) penalties in total. Where two penalties are owed, the club that was ready chooses how they are served — both at once, for a five-on-three power play, or one after the other, for two consecutive five-on-four power plays.","At ten (10) minutes past game time the game is a forfeit, recorded as a 1-0 win for the club that was ready. No individual player statistics are applied to any player from a forfeited game, so that no player's record carries statistics from a game that was not played. The game still counts toward each player's weekly appearance total under Rule 5.2, and that cannot be undone to restore availability. A club may concede a forfeit before puck drop if it knows it cannot field six.","A club that is late because of matchmaking trouble in its immediately preceding game is not penalized for that lateness. The exemption is lost where the club shows a clear lack of urgency in getting the next game started. The league office rules on any dispute, and repeated reliance on this exemption is reviewed under Chapter 7.","The ten-minute forfeit stands unless it is waived by a member of league staff who is not playing in, managing, or otherwise involved in the game in question. The two clubs may not waive it between themselves. The penalties owed for lateness are a different matter: the club that was ready may waive them, in whole or in part, if both clubs agree — that is a decision for the two clubs, made between them before the game starts, and the league office records the outcome rather than second-guessing it.","The league does not record double forfeits. If neither club fields a lineup and no forfeit is claimed, the game has no result — the commissioner voids it, and a voided game is kept out of the standings. A one-sided failure is a forfeit, not a reschedule; a reschedule (Rule 3.3) is available only when both clubs have a genuine connection problem. Disputes over a claimed forfeit go to the league office, which may order a replay when neither side has stream evidence.","A club that knows in advance it cannot play should pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3 rather than allow a forfeit. Repeated forfeits are referred to the league office for review under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"3.3","title":"Reschedules","paragraphs":["A game may be rescheduled only by written agreement of both clubs’ management, filed with and approved by the league office before the originally scheduled puck drop. The league office will not approve reschedules that push a game past the end of the week in which it was originally scheduled unless exceptional circumstances exist.","Where the clubs cannot agree, the game stands as scheduled. The league office may on its own authority move a game for league-wide technical outages, EA server failures, or other events outside the clubs' control."]}]},{"num":4,"title":"Game settings & servers","sections":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Official game configuration","paragraphs":["All CGHL games are played in EA Sports NHL 27 as 6v6 club games using the league-published settings sheet, which forms part of this rulebook by reference. Games played on any other settings are unofficial and will be replayed on correct settings at the league office's direction.","Regulation is three (3) periods of four (4) minutes each. A game tied at the end of regulation goes to overtime played five-on-five at full strength, in periods of the same four-minute length, sudden death — the first goal wins. Overtime periods are played back to back, continuously, until a team scores; CGHL does not use a shootout in any game, and no CGHL game can end in a tie.","The overtime format is the same in the regular season and the playoffs. A game decided in overtime is a win for the club that scores and an overtime loss for the club that does not; the points those results carry are set out in Rule 8.1.","The in-game clock displays each four-minute period as twenty (20) minutes, and the final minute of the third period runs in real time rather than at the accelerated rate. In the private lobby, each club selects the side shown on the league schedule — the club listed as Home selects Home, and the club listed as Away selects Away — so the recorded box score always matches the fixture."]},{"id":"4.2","title":"Private game codes and server selection","paragraphs":["League games are played in private lobbies secured by a game code issued by the league office. The codes for a game night are released together, thirty (30) minutes before the night's first scheduled puck drop, and only to the two clubs in each game — the players on their rosters and their front offices. No other member can see a code at any time; the league office can. The site enforces this release in the database, not on the page.","Game codes are confidential. A code may be shared within the two clubs that play the game, which is what the club room and the match card are for, and may never be posted publicly or given to anyone outside them — including in open Discord channels, streams, social media, or any other public venue. Publicly sharing a game code, or admitting a non-rostered player to a league lobby, is a conduct violation subject to discipline under Chapter 7, and the league office may order the game replayed on a fresh code.","Every completed game is checked against the two clubs' rosters, and against the position each player is rostered at. Where a player appears in a box score for a club that holds him on neither its active roster nor its training camp, or where a player in a box score cannot be identified as a member at all, the league office is notified and the matter is referred to the department that owns it. A club that dresses a player it does not hold has admitted a non-rostered player to a league lobby, and is dealt with under the paragraph above and under Chapter 7. Where a player on a club's active roster appears in a box score at a position outside his position group, that too is notified and referred. A player on an active roster is held to his group (Rule 2.1); a training-camp player fills any position. The check is made by group, because the game reports a defenseman without saying which side he played.","The league plays on four (4) servers: NA Northeast, NA Southeast, NA Central and NA West. The list is the commissioner's to set and is published here; no game may be played on a server outside it. Where neither club has named a server for a game, that game is played on the standard server, which is NA Central.","Each club names its own choices for each game from Team HQ, on the schedule desk. The club listed as Home names a first choice and a second choice. The club listed as Away names the one server it will not play on, its veto, and may also name a server it prefers. Each club's picks are private to that club; neither sees the other's before the night settles.","The server is then settled by the league, in this order: the Home club's first choice, unless the Away club vetoed it, in which case the Home club's second choice; where the Home club named nothing, the Away club's preferred server, provided that is not its own veto; and where neither club named anything that can be honored, the standard server, unless the standard server is the veto, in which case the first server on the list that is not the veto. A club that files nothing accepts the outcome that follows from the other club's picks and this paragraph.","Picks freeze for the whole night thirty (30) minutes before that night's first scheduled puck drop, which is the same moment the codes are released, and the server for each game of the night is settled then. A club may change its picks freely until that moment and not afterwards. On the day of each game night the league office reminds any club that has not yet filed, ninety (90) minutes before the night's first puck drop, one hour before the freeze.","At that moment the league office posts each club, in its own club room, a single notice setting out every game it plays that night: the time, the opponent, the server settled for that game, and the private lobby code. Nothing of that notice is sent before the night locks, because until then the codes are not released and the servers are not settled. A club that receives no such notice should not wait on it; the same information is on its schedule desk, and the office should be told.","Players may record the server they play best on in their site settings, and their club's management sees the roster's answers alongside the controls that set the picks. Those answers are advisory. The picks and the veto belong to the club's front office, and a club is not required to follow them."]},{"id":"4.3","title":"Disconnections","paragraphs":["If a player loses connection during play, that player's club leaves the game at the next whistle. Every statistic recorded between the disconnection and that whistle counts in full — for both clubs and for every player, whoever they are. Nothing earned in that window is struck from the record.","The clubs then reload and replay the game, and the club that lost the player takes one (1) penalty within one (1) minute of the game-clock time at which the disconnection occurred. The replayed game is thereby brought back into balance with the abandoned one.","However many sessions a game takes — the abandoned sitting and every reload — they are combined into a single game record through the league's stats import, so that the box score reads as one complete game and no player's statistics are divided across fragments.","A second disconnection by the same club in the same game is answered with two (2) penalties on the next reload. The club that did not disconnect chooses how they are served — both at once, for a five-on-three power play, or one after the other, for two consecutive five-on-four power plays.","League games are played on four-minute periods, which the in-game clock displays as twenty (20) minutes, and the final minute of the third period runs in real time rather than at the accelerated rate. A third-period disconnection therefore costs more real time than the game clock suggests. Where the disconnection occurs in the third period, the owed penalty is taken five (5) minutes of game clock earlier than the time of the disconnection, which accounts for that difference.","Where the disconnection occurs within the first five (5) minutes of the third period, the clubs do not replay a full game. They reload and play the entire first period of the new game as if it were the third period, and the club that disconnected takes its owed penalty or penalties as soon as possible after puck drop. Playing a whole period this way accounts for the additional real time carried by the final minute of a third period.","A third disconnection by the same club in the same game ends the game as a forfeit loss for that club. All statistics are retained: the sessions played are combined into a single game through the league's stats import, and the score recorded is the combined score standing at the third disconnection. The game is published as a forfeit loss — marked FFL on the result — and that ruling stands even where the forfeiting club finished with more goals; the goals themselves, and every individual line, are kept exactly as they were earned. This is the one forfeit that keeps its numbers: the game was played, and the players who played it keep what they did.","If the lobby itself fails — a full crash or an EA server outage affecting both clubs — captains must capture evidence of the score and game clock at failure. The league office will order the game resumed from the recorded score and time where feasible, and replayed in full where it is not. A club may not use a disconnect, real or feigned, to gain a stoppage, delay, or matchup advantage.","A game that is left unfinished is abandoned once either club plays a different opponent. An abandoned game is a forfeit: the club ahead at the moment play stopped is credited the win on the score as it stood, the club behind is recorded as forfeiting, and BOTH clubs keep every statistic their players earned in the sittings that were played. The league files this automatically when the record shows it — an unfinished game, and one of its clubs afterwards playing someone else — and the officials are told each time. Where the abandoned game was level, no club is ahead and the league office rules it; where the clubs did in fact intend to resume, the officials reverse the ruling and the sittings are merged as usual.","Late starts and disconnections are recorded against the club by the league office in the game incident log, which is how the second and third occurrences in this rule are counted."]},{"id":"4.4","title":"Connection standards","paragraphs":["Players are expected to compete on a stable connection. Persistent, game-affecting lag attributable to a single player may be reviewed by the league office, which may require the player to sit until the issue is resolved. Deliberate manipulation of one's connection to induce lag is cheating and is subject to discipline under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"4.5","title":"Positional builds and X-Factor restrictions","paragraphs":["CGHL is decided by skill, not by loadout. Every player dresses on a legal build for his position and keeps his X-Factors within the restrictions of this rule, which the league maintains so that games turn on how a player plays rather than on a chosen ability. This rule binds every game — regular season and playoffs. A player who loads into a game with a banned ability, a banned build, a pre-built loadout or a special-character cosmetic does not play that game as loaded: the game is restarted, and the offending player serves one (1) minor penalty at the start of the restarted game, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","The following X-Factor abilities are banned at every tier the game offers them: Truculence, Spark Plug, Hipster, Pressure+, Unstoppable Force and Big Rig. Two further abilities are banned at the Red tier only — Stick ’Em Up and Big Tipper — and may be equipped below it. The banned-ability and banned-build lists in this rule are written against NHL 27 and apply as written; the office keeps the definitive list with the settings sheet and may add to it at any time as the game is patched or the meta shifts, and any change is posted with that week’s schedule and in the changelog.","The league is played with standard created players only, built by hand. Every pre-built loadout the game offers is banned: no player shall dress on a ready-made build, whatever its name or position. Special-character player classes — the pre-built gimmick classes such as Moonlight, Hammer, Bones, Wally, Deepfreeze, Surge, and Vector — are banned, as is every special or mascot character, and every special-character cosmetic is banned with them: no item, skin or effect belonging to a special character may be worn on a created player. These names are illustrative, not exhaustive: no pre-built loadout, no special class, no special character and none of its cosmetics may be dressed in any CGHL game, at any position.","Builds are locked to their position group, and no enforcer build may be used at either position. A forward may not use the Enforcer (ENF) build, nor any defenseman build — Defensive Defenseman (DD), Offensive Defenseman (OD), Enforcer Defenseman (ENFD, tagged EFD in some menus), Two-Way Defenseman (TWD), or Puck-Moving Defenseman (PMD). A defenseman may not use the Enforcer Defenseman (ENFD) build, nor any forward build — Grinder (GRN), Playmaker (PLY), Sniper (SNP), Power Forward (PWF), Two-Way Forward (TWF), Enforcer (ENF), or Dangler (DNG). What remains is the legal set: a forward builds as a Sniper, Playmaker, Grinder, Power Forward, Two-Way Forward, or Dangler; a defenseman builds as a Defensive, Offensive, Two-Way, or Puck-Moving Defenseman. No build outside this legal set may be dressed at that position.","Responsibility for fielding legal builds rests with the player and his club’s management. Managers should confirm every dressed player’s build and abilities before lineups lock under Rule 5.3. When a banned ability, build, loadout or cosmetic is found after a game has loaded, either club may call for the restart, and the offending club shall restart; the penalty of this rule is served by the offending player as a minor penalty taken at the start of the restarted game and verified by the officials against the box score. A club that declines to restart forfeits the game under Rule 3.2. The league office maintains the banned-ability list with the settings sheet (Rule 4.1) and announces any change to it with that week’s schedule."]}]},{"num":5,"title":"Availability & lineups","sections":[{"id":"5.1","title":"Weekly availability","paragraphs":["Every rostered player must submit weekly availability for the upcoming game week through the league’s availability system — an answer of Available or Not Available for each of the club’s games that week, with an optional note per night — no later than 7:30 PM Eastern Time on the day the game week’s first games are played — Wednesday in a normal week, 90 minutes before the first puck drop (a holiday-shifted week keeps the same rule against its own first night). Availability submitted after the deadline is accepted at club management’s discretion for lineup purposes but is recorded as late by the league office — the system stamps the time of every answer itself and marks a post-deadline answer late; the record cannot be back-dated.","Club management is responsible for confirming that the club’s availability picture supports an eligible lineup for each scheduled game. A club that discovers an availability shortfall after the deadline must pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3 without delay; waiting until game night is not a defense to a forfeit.","Availability informs the club; it does not bind the lineup. Club management may dress any eligible rostered player in any game regardless of the availability he stated, and the league's lineup tools show each player's stated availability beside his name and flag a player marked not available when he is placed, rather than refusing him. A club that dresses a player who stated he could not play bears the consequences under Rule 3.2 if he does not appear; the player is not at fault for a game he said he could not play.","The league sets no weekly availability quota beyond the appearance cap of Rule 5.2: a player may mark himself available for as many or as few of the week's games as he can play. A club whose availability picture falls short of icing an eligible lineup must pursue a reschedule under Rule 3.3. Twenty-four (24) hours before the window closes, the league office posts a reminder in each club’s room naming the players whose answers are not yet in, Lineups are set day by day rather than for the week at once: on each game day the office asks each club’s front office to have that night’s lineups filed by the time the night’s first game locks, so that a club sets its lines on the day, with the week’s availability finished in front of it, and the office posts a reminder that afternoon. Both requests are courtesies of the office and not deadlines in themselves, and the lineup lock is unchanged, falling thirty (30) minutes before each game under Rule 5.3. When the window closes, the league office notifies every rostered player whose availability is not in, on the site and by Discord message, and tells his club; a rostered player who does not submit availability at all in consecutive weeks is reviewed by the league office under Chapter 7. The commissioner may impose a floor for an unusual week by announcing it with that week's schedule."]},{"id":"5.2","title":"Eligible lineups","paragraphs":["A lineup is eligible when every dressed player is on the club's confirmed roster, is not under suspension, is dressed at a position permitted by his assignment under Rule 2.1, is within his weekly appearance cap under this rule, and satisfies the position requirements of the settings sheet, including a rostered goaltender. Dressing an ineligible player results in forfeiture of the game in which the player dressed, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","Every dressed player is subject to a weekly appearance cap in the regular season, counted across the game-week in which the game falls. No active-roster player — skater or goaltender — may be dressed in more than six (6) games in a game-week. A club plays nine (9) games in a full game-week, three on each of Wednesday, Thursday and Friday. A player's games in a game-week are counted from the official record (Chapter 6) for every game played, and from the lineups his club has filed for every game still to be played; a game that is voided counts for no one. The cap is enforced when a lineup is filed: a lineup that would place a player beyond it is refused, and the league's lineup tools refuse to assign him to a further game once he has reached it. Every box score is read against the week when a game is made final: a player who appears beyond the cap, or who appears without having been filed in his club's lineup, is reported to the league office automatically and dealt with under Rule 5.3. In the playoffs the cap of Rule 8.3 applies in its place.","Training-camp players are subject to a cap of three (3) games in a game-week and may be dressed at any position regardless of their declared position. Rostered players shall be given preference over training-camp players wherever a rostered player is available for the game; a club that dresses a camp player in place of an available rostered player may be required to account for it to the league office.","Position assignment operates by group. A rostered forward may be dressed at any forward position — center, left wing or right wing — and a rostered defenseman at either defense position. A goaltender may be dressed only in goal. A training-camp player may be dressed anywhere. The league's lineup tools apply these groups when a lineup is filed, not only when it is built, and where a club dresses a camp player while a rostered player of that group is both under his weekly cap and available for the night, the tools say so before the lineup is filed.","A forfeited game counts toward these totals under Rule 3.2 and cannot be reversed to restore a player's availability."],"full":["A lineup is eligible when every dressed player is on the club’s confirmed roster, is not under suspension, is dressed at a position his assignment permits under Rule 2.1, is within his weekly game cap below, and satisfies the position requirements of the settings sheet, including a rostered goaltender. Dressing an ineligible player results in forfeiture of the game in which the player dressed, without prejudice to further discipline under Chapter 7.","Every dressed player is subject to a weekly appearance cap in the regular season and the playoffs, counted across the game week in which the game falls. A skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games in a week; a goaltender in at most six (6). The cap does not apply in the pre-season, where a club may dress any rostered or camp player as often as it needs, and where its Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position — pre-season rosters are still filling, and no club should forfeit a warm-up game for want of a body. A club plays nine (9) games in a full week — three on each of Wednesday, Thursday and Friday — so a club's nine forwards and six defensemen exactly cover its nine games, and its two goaltenders cover them with room to spare. The cap is enforced when the lineup is set: a lineup that would put a player past it is refused.","Training-camp players are capped at three (3) games a week on the same terms, and may be dressed at any position regardless of their listed one. Rostered players must be given preference over training-camp players wherever a rostered player is available for the game. A club that dresses a camp player in place of an available rostered player may be asked to account for it by the league office.","Position assignment runs by group. A rostered forward may be dressed at any forward position — center, left wing or right wing — and a rostered defenseman at either defense position. Goaltenders may be dressed only in goal. A training-camp player may be dressed anywhere. In pre-season games, so may the club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager; the exception ends with the pre-season, and a saved line that carries a manager out of his group will not dress into a regular-season or playoff game. The league’s lineup tools apply these groups when a lineup is filed, not only when it is built. Where a club dresses a camp player while a rostered player of that group is both under his weekly cap and available for the night, the league's lineup tools say so before the lineup is filed.","A forfeited game counts toward these totals under Rule 3.2 and cannot be undone to restore a player's availability."]},{"id":"5.3","title":"Lineup lock","paragraphs":["A club may withdraw a lineup it has filed at any time before that game locks, which returns the game to every player on it for the purposes of Rule 5.2; a game left unfiled at puck drop exposes the club under Rule 3.2. Lineups lock thirty (30) minutes before the scheduled puck drop. The lock publishes the sheet: from that moment the opposing club can see it, so that each club knows roughly who it faces. It does not close the sheet. A club may still change its lineup after the lock, at no cost and without the league office's permission, provided the player brought in is on that club's active roster or in its training camp and the lineup continues to satisfy the position rules of Rule 2.1 and the appearance limits of Rule 5.2. The system accepts a change until ten (10) minutes after the scheduled puck drop; from then the game is under way and who actually played is read from the box score.","A change made after the lock costs the club nothing. It is, however, reported automatically the moment it is made to the opposing club's front office, on the site and in its club room, naming the players who came out and the players who went in, and it is marked on the game's matchup page. That reporting exists for one reason: the sheet the opponent is looking at must be the current one. A club that changes its lineup late is not doing anything irregular and is not reviewed for it.","A player is notified the moment his club dresses him for a game, and again if it later takes him off that game's sheet, so that no member has to go looking to learn whether he is playing. Every rostered player can also see the club’s set lineups for the week from My Hub, which six are dressed for each game, as management sets them. For statistics under Chapter 6 and for eligibility review the record is the box score, not the filed sheet: the sheet is a forecast and the box score is what happened. A player appearing in a box score for a club that holds him on neither its active roster nor its training camp, or appearing outside his position group, is reported automatically to the league office and dealt with under Rules 4.2, 2.1 and 5.2 as the case requires. That a player was not on the filed sheet is not, by itself, a matter for review."]}]},{"num":6,"title":"Statistics & verification","sections":[{"id":"6.1","title":"The official record","paragraphs":["The league's published standings, statistics, and transaction log constitute the official record of the CGHL. In any conflict between the official record and a club's own records, EA's in-game screens, or third-party trackers, the official record controls once the league office has verified the game under this chapter."]},{"id":"6.2","title":"Automatic import and evidence","paragraphs":["Scores and full box scores import automatically from the EA NHL match record after every league game — no club submits results by hand. For a game to import, it must be played in the correct private lobby (the game's 6-digit code) between the two clubs' linked EA clubs, and it must finish inside that fixture's game window — from ten (10) minutes before its scheduled puck drop to three (3) hours after. The league's poller looks only during those windows and only for the clubs scheduled in them, and the importer files only a match between the two scheduled clubs that ended inside the window; a scrimmage between the same clubs earlier that day, a rematch after the night, or a game played against a club other than the scheduled opponent is never imported as the league game. A disconnected game's replay under Rule 4.3 is merged into the sitting it continues, never filed as a new game, and a completed game is never resumed. A game that genuinely finished outside its window — a night that ran long past three hours — is filed by the league's statistics staff from the club game stats desk, not by the robot. The exceptions are the games the automatic record cannot cover: a forfeit has no EA match to import and is entered by the league office under Rule 3.2, and a lagged-out game's sessions are combined into one record through the league's stats tools — by the winning club's management or by statistics staff (Rule 4.3).","Clubs should still capture end-of-game screenshots or stream footage as evidence. If an import fails or attributes a game incorrectly, the league office replays the archived EA match data or re-opens the game; evidence resolves any dispute the archive cannot. Repeated play outside the assigned lobby — which breaks the automatic import — is subject to discipline under Chapter 7."]},{"id":"6.3","title":"Corrections and disputes","paragraphs":["A club that believes the official record is wrong must file a correction request with the league office, identifying the game and the specific entry disputed and attaching supporting evidence. The league office rules on correction requests and its statistical rulings are final, except that a ruling which itself imposes discipline may be appealed under Rule 7.6.","A box score line belongs to the club that played it. Where the league office withdraws an individual's credit for a line, whether because the player has left the league or because the line cannot be attributed to any member with confidence, the line stays in the club's box score and the game keeps its score; only the personal credit is removed. A withdrawal is recorded against the game and the EA account it concerns, survives any later re-import of that game, and may be reversed by the league office on the same record. Withdrawing credit is an administrative correction and is not itself discipline.","Statistics accrued through verified manipulation — including arranged goals, deliberate own-goals, or padding against a complicit opponent — will be struck from the record, and the players and clubs involved referred for discipline under Chapter 7."]}]},{"num":7,"title":"Discipline, suspensions & appeals","sections":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Disciplinary authority","paragraphs":["The league office holds exclusive authority to investigate suspected violations of this rulebook and to impose discipline on players, club management, and clubs. The league office may act on a filed complaint, an official’s report, or its own observation, and may review any league game in full, including recorded footage and Discord communications in league spaces.","A complaint is routed to the department that must act on it, and that department is notified the moment one is filed: complaints about conduct on the ice — and appeals of rulings — go to the officiating department, while complaints about conduct in the Discord go to the community department, whose moderators may issue warnings and escalate anything heavier. Every official can see every filing, including a complaint about a commissioner or a member of league staff. What is restricted is authority, not visibility: no official may rule on a case they filed or a case about themselves, so a complaint naming an official is decided by someone else. The substance of a complaint is never published to a staff channel — only that one was filed, and under which heading; officials open the case itself to read it.","Discipline is corrective, not merely punitive: sanctions are set to protect competitive integrity and member safety, with the player's history and the harm caused weighed in every decision."]},{"id":"7.2","title":"Classes of sanction","paragraphs":["Available sanctions include, in escalating order: formal warning, single-game suspension, multi-game suspension, forfeiture of games or statistics, suspension for the remainder of the season including playoffs, and expulsion from the league. Sanctions may be combined where a single course of conduct violates multiple rules. League staff may impose suspensions of up to ten (10) games or thirty (30) days; suspension for the remainder of the season, expulsion, and any sanction against a commissioner or staff member are imposed only by a commissioner. A staff official who believes a case warrants more than their ceiling leaves the case open and escalates it.","A suspended player may not dress, appear in a league lobby, or serve as a club's point of contact for game operations while the suspension runs. Suspensions carry across season boundaries where unserved games remain."]},{"id":"7.3","title":"In-game misconduct","paragraphs":["Conduct penalized within the game by EA's rule engine is ordinarily punished by the game itself. The league office layers supplemental discipline on top of in-game penalties where the conduct is dangerous, repeated, or targeted — the fact that a penalty was served in-game does not exhaust the league's disciplinary interest in it."]},{"id":"7.4","title":"Dangerous contact","paragraphs":["Dangerous contact is any deliberate act whose evident purpose is to injure, intimidate, or provoke rather than to play the puck. It expressly includes checking an opponent from behind — particularly into the boards or the goal frame — and all post-whistle misconduct, including hits, cross-checks, slew-foots, and goaltender contact delivered after play has been blown dead.","The baseline sanction for a verified act of dangerous contact is a two (2) game suspension. The league office adjusts upward from the baseline for aggravating factors — targeting a goaltender, repetition within the same game, prior history under this rule, or conduct that continues after a warning — and may reduce below the baseline only in documented, exceptional circumstances. Effective version 1.2 of this rulebook, the baseline is mandatory: a verified violation of this rule may not be resolved by warning alone.","Club management shares responsibility under this rule. A manager who directs, encourages, or conspicuously tolerates dangerous contact by their players is subject to discipline as if they had committed the act."]},{"id":"7.5","title":"Repeat offenses","paragraphs":["A player's full disciplinary history in the CGHL is before the league office in every matter. Repeat violations of the same rule presumptively draw a longer suspension than the prior sanction for that rule, and a pattern of violations across different rules may itself justify a season-length suspension or expulsion even where no single incident would."]},{"id":"7.6","title":"Appeals","paragraphs":["A player or club sanctioned under this chapter may appeal by filing a written appeal with the league office within forty-eight (48) hours of the sanction being posted. The appeal must identify the ruling appealed, state the grounds — factual error, misapplication of a rule, or disproportionate sanction — and attach any evidence not already before the league office. Sanctions not appealed within the 48-hour window are final.","Appeals are decided by a panel that excludes any official who imposed the original sanction. Filing an appeal does not stay a suspension: the sanction runs while the appeal is pending, and games already served are credited if the appeal succeeds. The panel may uphold, reduce, or vacate a sanction; it may increase a sanction only where the appeal itself reveals misconduct not previously known."]}]},{"num":8,"title":"Playoffs & seeding","sections":[{"id":"8.1","title":"Qualification and seeding","paragraphs":["At the conclusion of the regular season the top three (3) clubs in each division qualify for the CGHL playoffs, producing a six-club field. Clubs are seeded one through three within their own division by regular-season record. Each division plays its own bracket: the first seed receives a bye through the opening round while the second seed meets the third; the winner meets the first seed for the division championship; and the two division champions meet in the final.","Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not change between rounds.","Clubs are ordered by total points earned. A regulation or overtime win is worth two (2) points, an overtime loss one (1), and a regulation loss none. The table is not adjusted for games in hand: a club that has played more games may stand higher on points alone, and the club behind it retains the opportunity to pass it by playing the games it is owed. A forfeit counts for points as an ordinary result — two points to the club awarded the win and none to the club that forfeited.","Clubs are assigned to the East and West divisions by the league office and published with the season schedule; assignments hold for the full season."],"full":["At the conclusion of the regular season, the top four (4) clubs in each division qualify for the CGHL playoffs, producing an eight-club field. Clubs are seeded one through four inside their own division by regular-season record. Each division plays its own bracket: the first seed meets the fourth and the second meets the third, the winners meet for the division title, and the two division champions meet in the final.","Seeding is fixed when the league office certifies the final standings and does not re-seed between rounds.","Clubs are ordered by total points earned. A regulation or overtime win is worth two points, an overtime loss one, and a regulation loss none. The table is not adjusted for games in hand: a club that has played more games may stand higher on points alone, and the club behind it holds the chance to pass it by playing the games it is owed. A forfeit counts for points exactly as an ordinary result, a win worth two points to the club that was ready and a loss worth none to the club that forfeited.","Clubs are assigned to the East and West divisions by the league office and published with the season schedule; assignments hold for the full season."]},{"id":"8.2","title":"Tiebreakers","paragraphs":["Ties in the standings — for division titles, playoff berths, or seeding — are broken in the following order: head-to-head record between the tied clubs; regulation wins; goal differential across all regular-season games; goals scored; and, if still tied, a one-game play-in scheduled by the league office. Forfeited games count as regulation results for all tiebreaker purposes.","A forfeit is a regulation result and never awards the overtime-loss point. Where a forfeit keeps its played score under Rule 4.3, the goals scored in it count normally toward goal differential and goals scored, even for the club that forfeited — the ruling decides the win and the loss, not the goals."]},{"id":"8.3","title":"Playoff play","paragraphs":["Playoff rounds are contested as best-of-seven series played within a single game-week in a 2-2-3 format: two games on Wednesday, two on Thursday, and up to three on Friday as required. The higher seed holds home designation for scheduling and reporting purposes. A club that wins four (4) games advances immediately, and the remaining games of the series are canceled. A division's first seed plays no series in the opening week; its postseason begins with the division championship in the following game-week.","All regular-season rules apply in the playoffs except where this rulebook or the published playoff format expressly provides otherwise. Overtime follows the continuous sudden-death format of the regular season under Rule 4.1: there are no shootouts, and a tied game plays successive overtime periods until it is decided.","A playoff appearance cap applies in place of the weekly cap of Rule 5.2: no player — skater, goaltender or training-camp player — may be dressed in more than four (4) games of a single series. The roster composition published under Rule 2.1 is sized so that a full active roster can ice an eligible lineup for all seven games of a series. Dressing a player beyond the cap is treated as dressing an ineligible player under Rule 5.2 and forfeits the game.","The commissioner shall determine, for each season, the number of regular-season games a player must have appeared in to be dressed in the playoffs (the playoff eligibility floor) and shall publish it with the season settings before that season's draft; the figure published for a season is binding for its duration. A player is eligible to be dressed in the playoffs only if he has appeared in at least that number of regular-season games for a club in that season, was on the club's active roster or training camp at the movement deadline under Rule 2.4, and is not under suspension for the game in question. Appearances are counted from the official record (Chapter 6) across every club the player was rostered with in the season. The floor in force appears on the Seasons page. The league's lineup tools refuse a playoff lineup containing a player short of it, and Team HQ shows each club's players against it through the regular season so that management may distribute games accordingly. A goaltender signed under the emergency provision of Rule 2.4 is playoff-eligible notwithstanding the floor and his date of joining; the provision exists for that case."],"full":["Playoff rounds are contested as best-of-seven series played inside a single game week in a 2-2-3 format: two games Wednesday, two Thursday, and three Friday if they are needed. The higher seed holds home designation for scheduling and reporting purposes. A club that wins four (4) games advances immediately, and the remaining games of the series are canceled.","All regular-season rules apply in the playoffs except where this rulebook or the published playoff format expressly provides otherwise. Overtime follows the same continuous, sudden-death format as the regular season under Rule 4.1 — there are no shootouts, and a tied game plays successive overtime periods until it is decided.","The weekly appearance cap of Rule 5.2 applies in the playoffs without change: a skater may be dressed in at most three (3) games of a series, a goaltender in at most six (6). With nine forwards and six defensemen on the active roster, a club can ice a full lineup for all seven games. Dressing a player past his cap is treated as dressing an ineligible player under Rule 5.2 and forfeits the game. There is no games-played floor for playoff eligibility; the player must have been on the club's active roster at the movement deadline under Rule 2.4 and must not be under suspension for the game in question. A goaltender signed under the emergency exception of Rule 2.4 is playoff-eligible despite joining after the movement deadline — the exception exists precisely for that case."]}]},{"num":9,"title":"Awards & honors","sections":[{"id":"9.1","title":"League awards","paragraphs":["The CGHL confers end-of-season awards recognizing individual and club excellence, including most valuable player, best goaltender, best defenseman, and the league championship trophy awarded to the playoff champion. The full slate of awards for a season is published by the league office before the final week of the regular season."]},{"id":"9.2","title":"Selection and voting","paragraphs":["Individual awards are decided by a vote the league office runs among league staff and club management, informed by the official statistical record under Chapter 6. No voter may cast a first-place vote for a member of their own club. The league office publishes voting results with the award announcements.","Statistical titles — scoring leaders and goaltending leaders — are determined directly from the official record and are not subject to vote. Minimum-appearance thresholds for goaltending titles are published with the settings sheet."]},{"id":"9.3","title":"Eligibility and forfeiture of honors","paragraphs":["A player serving a suspension of season length or longer is ineligible for individual awards for that season. The league office may strip an award or honor, before or after conferral, where the underlying performance is found to have involved a verified violation of Chapter 6 or Chapter 7."]}]},{"num":10,"title":"Definitions","sections":[{"id":"10.1","title":"Terms of art","paragraphs":["As used in this rulebook: “league office” means the CGHL commissioner and staff acting in their official capacity; “club” means a member team of the CGHL together with its management and rostered players; “club management” means a club’s Owner, General Manager, and Assistant General Manager (Rule 2.6); “puck drop” means the scheduled start time of a game as published on the official schedule; “dress” means to appear in a league lobby as a participant in a game; “game week” means the scheduling week as delineated on the official schedule; and “settings sheet” means the league-published game configuration document incorporated by Rule 4.1.","\"Eligible lineup\" carries the meaning given in Rule 5.2. \"Dangerous contact\" carries the meaning given in Rule 7.4. \"Official record\" carries the meaning given in Rule 6.1. “Standard server” means the server on which a game is played where neither club has named one, as set out in Rule 4.2. “Veto” means the one server the Away club declares it will not play on for a given game (Rule 4.2).","“Season format” means the model under which a season is conducted, designated by the commissioner for each season and stated at the head of this rulebook: the basic format, which is the league standard and the text of these chapters, or the full format, preserved in Appendix A. “Season settings” means the figures the commissioner determines and publishes for each season before its draft under Rules 2.1, 2.8 and 8.3 — the size and composition of the active roster (which may be stated as complete lines plus additional players of any position), any training-camp limit, the number of draft rounds, and the playoff eligibility floor. “Minimum service” means the three (3) regular-season appearances in the current season a player must have before his club may waive or trade him (Rule 2.4). “Depth” or “depth placement” means a player placed on a club by the league office under Rule 2.8, who is the club’s player in every respect and is carried in its training camp; called up, he counts against the roster composition of Rule 2.1 like any other player."]},{"id":"10.2","title":"Interpretation and amendment","paragraphs":["Chapter and section headings are for convenience and do not limit the text. Where this rulebook is silent, the league office rules in the manner that best protects competitive integrity, and such rulings have the force of the rulebook until it is amended. Time references are Eastern Time unless stated otherwise, and stated time periods include weekends and holidays.","This rulebook may be amended by the league office at any time. Amendments are published with a version number and effective date, take effect prospectively from the stated date, and do not reopen games or rulings completed under a prior version except where the amendment expressly says so."]}]}],"changelog":[{"version":"3.19","dateIso":"2026-09-25","summary":"The box score lines that could not be matched to a member have been linked, and every game of the season was re-checked against the roster, camp and position rules afterwards. Twelve of the thirteen unmatched lines now sit on the right profile. Seven games looked like rule breaks and all seven turn out to be legal, including two Seattle games that had never been raised at all. One line is still unidentified and is with the officials.","items":["Twelve box score lines across six members are now linked to their profiles, so those stats appear on the player pages and in the leaders. Each was matched on the EA ID the member registered, and only where exactly one member could possibly be meant.","Those six members also had their EA account ids recorded, so none of them will ever have to be matched on a name again. Eighty seven members are now recognized by account rather than by spelling.","Game limits were checked across the whole season and no player has gone over his weekly cap.","Two Seattle games where a defenseman played wing turned out to be legal: he was in the training camp at the time, which was never flagged because the check reads a squad as it stands today rather than as it stood that night. The club notices gave the exact timestamps.","A ruling on a player who is no longer on the roster he played for now sticks. Before today the officials were paged again about the same game every time the check ran, because the answer had nowhere to be recorded.","One Dallas goaltender line from the Tuesday 9:00 PM game still matches no member. Dallas dressed a different six than it filed, which is allowed, so there is no way to work out who it was and guessing is not on the table. It is on the officials' desk."]},{"version":"3.18","dateIso":"2026-09-25","summary":"A game that disconnected part way through is no longer posted as a result. Seattle against Pittsburgh went out last night as a 2 to 3 loss from a sitting that had only played 40 minutes, and the rest of the game arrived twelve minutes later and made it 4 to 3 the other way. The winner had been announced wrongly and nothing ever corrected it. A sitting that has not reached the end of regulation is now held until the rest of it turns up, and a score that changes after a game has gone final is announced.","items":["A sitting short of a full 60 minutes is held. The box score is recorded but no result is published, so nothing reaches the standings or the scores channel until the game is whole.","When the rest of the game arrives it merges as before and only then does the game go final, with both sittings added together.","If the rest never arrives, the game goes to the statistics desk once its window closes. It was abandoned rather than disconnected and someone has to rule on it.","A score that changes after a game has gone final is now announced as a correction. Until today a late fix was silent and the channel kept the first number.","The Seattle against Pittsburgh score has been corrected in the scores channel by hand, since it happened before this change."]},{"version":"3.17","dateIso":"2026-09-25","summary":"A player removed from a roster is now kept on file, so putting him back is a lookup rather than a reconstruction. Until today a waiver deleted the roster spot outright and his position, salary, number, squad and how he arrived all went with it. Three Utah waivers had to be reversed by hand this week and every one of those values had to be pieced together from old club notices; one player's number was never recorded anywhere and he came back wearing a different one.","items":["Every roster removal is archived whole, with the reason and who did it, whatever removed it: a club waiving somebody, the league office, or the rule that removes a player who leaves the Discord.","A removal with no reason recorded is still archived, because an unexplained removal is the one most worth being able to look up.","The league office can reinstate from that record, and the player comes back at the same position, salary, number, squad and origin.","A training camp player comes back to training camp, not to the active roster.","A removal cannot be reinstated twice, and a player already back on a roster is refused."]},{"version":"3.16","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Placing a late sign-up is a straight draw now. It used to go to whichever club was carrying the fewest players, which is why two in a row landed on the same club: that club had waived a player the night before and was genuinely the thinnest, then tied thinnest. Roster size no longer enters it. One thing still jumps the queue: a club with an empty training camp is taken first, so no club is ever left with no depth at all.","items":["A late sign-up now goes to a club chosen at random, with no weighting by roster size.","A club whose training camp is completely empty is taken first. Once every club has at least one camp player, that stops applying and the draw is purely random.","Only late arrivals changed. The pre-season pass that spreads the whole registration pool across the league still fills the emptiest clubs first, because that is a different job.","Checked before shipping: 4,000 simulated draws across the eight clubs came out between 11.8 and 13.5 percent each, against an even share of 12.5."]},{"version":"3.15","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Lobby codes no longer come out reading as something crude. Codes are six random digits, and nothing stopped those digits landing as 69, 420, 666 or a handful of other combinations. A single list now rules them out, used both by the thing that issues codes and by the thing that checks them, so the two can never drift apart. Of the 216 codes already issued, 22 matched the list and 19 were reissued. The other three were left alone on purpose.","items":["Ruled out anywhere in a code: 69, 420, 666, 88, 8008, 911, 187 and 1312.","Pickup lobby codes from the Discord bot get the same treatment, since they are read out the same way.","Three existing codes were deliberately not changed: two games that have already been played, where the record stands, and one whose night had already locked, because both clubs already had that code and changing it would send them to a lobby that does not exist.","The reissue refuses those two cases by itself rather than relying on anyone to remember them.","This costs about 11 percent of the possible codes and leaves close to 800,000, for a league that uses 450 a season.","The change log records the code that was replaced and not the new one, so it never becomes a second place a live code can be read."]},{"version":"3.14","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Every player now hears it on the site whenever he starts or stops being scheduled for a game. Being dressed and being taken off a sheet were already sent. Two other ways a member could come off a lineup were silent: his club taking the whole sheet down, which undressed all six without telling any of them, and being pulled from filed lineups after leaving the roster, where the club was told and the player was not. A player kept on the sheet but moved to a different position is told too, because what he is scheduled to do has changed.","items":["Your club files a sheet: you are told you are dressed, as before.","Your club takes the sheet down: all six are told they are no longer dressed. This was silent before.","You come off the roster: you are told about each filed lineup you were removed from, and only those. Your club was already told.","You are moved to a different position on the same sheet: you are told which position you are now playing.","A save that moves nobody still sends nothing, so nothing here adds noise.","Checked rather than assumed: lineups have exactly three writers in the database, and the site cannot write one directly, so every way of starting or stopping being scheduled is covered."]},{"version":"3.13","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Each club's own room is now told when its lineup is set, changed or withdrawn. Until today the players being dressed were told, and the opposing club was told about a change made after the lock, but the club's own front office and room heard nothing: management could file a sheet, swap a winger or take the sheet down and nobody in the room would see it. The notice names the fixture, the time, who came out, who went in, and prints the sheet in rink order.","items":["A sheet being filed posts Lineup set, with all six named in rink order.","A change posts Lineup changed, naming who came out and who went in, and the sheet as it now stands.","Moving the same six players between positions counts as a change and says so.","Pressing save without moving anybody says nothing, so the room stays quiet.","Taking a sheet down posts Lineup withdrawn, and notes that those games go back to every player on it for the weekly limit.","Withdrawing a sheet that was never filed says nothing.","No role is pinged. A lineup being filed is routine, and it lands in the room and in the front office's notifications."]},{"version":"3.12","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Tables sort by clicking a column heading now, and the old per column filter is gone. It hid behind a small funnel in the last heading and opened a whole row of text boxes, one per column, to answer a question a click on a heading answers. Every heading on the site is a sort control instead, and the filter is a single search box across the table. A position column sorts in rink order, not alphabetically. Separately, positions now read top to bottom as LW, C, RW, LD, RD, G everywhere a list of players is grouped that way.","items":["Click any column heading to sort. Click it again to reverse. Headings are reachable from the keyboard and announce the sort to screen readers.","A POS column sorts in rink order (LW, C, RW, LD, RD, G), never alphabetically. Numbers sort as numbers, including money and records like 2-1-0. Blanks always sink to the bottom.","A table with section rows, like a roster split into active and training camp, sorts inside each section and keeps the headings where they are.","The filter is one search box for the whole table instead of one box per column.","Two roster lists were sorting positions alphabetically, which put the goaltender second, and the Control Center opened every club on its goaltenders. All three now read in rink order.","Stat leaderboards are untouched: a list ranked by a statistic keeps its ranking, and you can click a heading if you want it another way.","Nine headings on the Team HQ roster table advertised sorting and did nothing at all. They work now."]},{"version":"3.11","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Leaving the league Discord now costs a rostered player his spot, after his club has been given a day to reach him. Until today the rule only covered players who had not yet been placed on a club; anyone already on a roster or in a training camp was exempt. When the league sees that a rostered member has left the server, a notice now goes to his club's room with the management role on it, naming him and the deadline. He has 24 hours from that notice to rejoin. If he does not, his sign-up is revoked and he comes off the squad. The club is told because the league itself cannot tell him: Discord does not deliver a message to someone who has left the server.","items":["Rule 1.1: a rostered or training camp player who leaves the Discord has 24 hours from the notice to rejoin, or his sign-up is revoked and he is removed from the club.","The clock starts at the notice, never at the moment he left, so no club loses a player before it has had a full day to reach him.","The notice goes to the club room and pings the management role, because the club can reach people the league cannot.","Rejoining inside the window cancels everything, and the club is told he is back.","A player holding an Owner, GM or AGM seat is never removed automatically. The league office is told and decides.","Club notices can now carry a role mention. Every existing notice is unchanged and still posts without pinging anyone."]},{"version":"3.10","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"The schedule and results lists were redrawn. A game row used to stretch the full width of the page with the matchup bunched in the middle: measured on the club schedule, more than half of each row was empty space, the rows were taller than they needed to be, and the score sat flush against its club's name so Mammoth 0 read as one word. A game is now a scoreboard, two clubs flanking one score, and a wide screen shows two games side by side instead of one very wide row. On a phone each club takes its own line with its score to the right.","items":["Every game row is the same height, and the crests, scores and status badges line up in columns down the list.","The score sits between the two clubs instead of against one club's name.","Wide screens show two games side by side. The column count follows the space available, so a narrow window or a phone simply gets one.","The losing score is dimmed so a result reads at a glance. A tie leaves both level.","Rows are shorter, so more of the schedule fits on screen at once.","On phones each club takes its own line with its score right aligned, which is how a scores app reads."]},{"version":"3.09","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Game problems now reach the staff once per game instead of once per player. Every check a finished game runs used to raise its own alert the moment it found something, and each one both notified every commissioner and officiating staffer and pinged the staff room, so a single messy game could arrive as three pings and twenty one notifications. The checks are unchanged. They are now gathered and sent as one notice per game, listing everything found under the fixture and its start time. The statistics room is also no longer told to enter games by hand: the automatic import is working, and the desk already has the tools.","items":["One notice per game, listing every finding, instead of one alert per player per finding.","The staff room is pinged once for that notice rather than once per finding.","What is still worth interrupting for: a player outside his position group, a player over the weekly game limit, a player the league cannot match to any member, and a player on neither the roster nor the training camp.","A club that filed no lineup is still recorded for the desk, but no longer raises an alert. The filed sheet has been a preview since the last update.","Weekly limit findings now go to the officiating desk in the same notice. Commissioners still receive it, as they receive every department notice.","The statistics room no longer gets an EA import alert pointing at manual entry. Anything unmatched is still listed on its desk, where the tools to fix it are.","Each finding is still written to the record, so nothing is lost and a repeat import cannot raise the same thing twice."]},{"version":"3.08","dateIso":"2026-09-24","summary":"Three records fixes after the first game night. The league office can now withdraw a player's personal credit for a box score line while the club keeps the line and the game keeps its score, which is what was asked for the Dallas player who left partway through the Canucks game. A compromised duplicate account that was holding a member's entire night of stats has been removed and those stats handed to his live account. And the EA archive was found to have never once recorded that an import succeeded: every clean import on the first night sat marked as needing a manual match, which is why the statistics desk kept being told to enter games that were already in.","items":["Rule 6.3: a box score line belongs to the club. Personal credit can be withdrawn on its own, and the line, the club totals and the score all stand.","A withdrawal is recorded against the game and the EA account, so re-importing that game cannot quietly hand the credit back. It can be reversed the same way.","The Dallas player who left during the Canucks game keeps his two complete games; his twenty minutes in the third are now the club's alone.","A banned duplicate account, flagged as compromised, was holding one member's 8 goals and 5 assists from the night. His live account now holds them, along with his EA account id so it cannot happen again.","The EA archive never recorded a successful import: the status write left out a required column, so it failed every single time and the error was swallowed.","Ten clean imports had been sitting marked as unmatched, which the statistics desk reads as work to do by hand. All ten are corrected, and a lost status is now reported instead of hidden."]},{"version":"3.06","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"Two things went wrong on the first game night and both are fixed. A waived player was being handed straight back to the club that released him, within seconds, by the sweep that seats undrafted players after the draft: it could not tell a released player from an undrafted one. A waiver is recorded now, and a waived player goes to the free agent board and stays there until a club signs him. Separately, the score of a game played across two sessions was read from the game's own disconnect placeholder rather than from what the players actually scored, which filed a tie that the rules do not allow. A score is the sum of its players' goals now, which is what every clean game of the night already agreed on.","items":["A waived player goes to the free agent pool and stays there. Any club can sign him from the free agents tab in Team HQ (Rule 2.2).","The post-draft placement sweep no longer picks up released players. It is for the undrafted class and nothing else.","Dallas's waived player has been returned to the pool, and the placements and contracts the loop created were undone. His stats are untouched and still his.","A game's score is now the sum of its players' goals. On every clean game of the first night the two already agreed exactly.","They only part company on a disconnected session, where the game reports its 3-0 did-not-finish line instead of the play. That filed Canucks at Stars as a 3-3 tie.","That game is corrected to Vancouver 6, Dallas 2, rebuilt from its own box score, which was right all along."]},{"version":"3.05","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The commissioner has settled what a filed lineup is for. It is a forecast, published to your opponent thirty minutes before puck drop so each club knows roughly who it faces. It is not a promise. A club that needs a last-minute switch may make one, free, right up to puck drop, provided the player coming in is on its roster or in its camp and the six still satisfy the position rules and the appearance limits. The penalty for a late change is abolished, and so is the emergency call-up ceremony that existed to warn about it. What is still enforced is who may play, which is checked against the box score: a player his club does not hold, or a player outside his position group, is still reported.","items":["A late lineup change costs nothing. The in-game penalty per player changed is gone.","There is no emergency call-up any more. The sheet simply stays editable until the game is under way.","The lock now publishes the sheet to the opponent rather than closing it to its owner.","Your opponent is still told when you change, so the lineup they are looking at is the current one. It is reported as information, not as an offence.","Position locks, the roster and camp requirement, and the weekly and series limits are unchanged and are still checked on every filing, including a late one.","The flag for playing off the filed sheet is retired. It fired 13 times on the first game night and 11 were legal switches; what it was really guarding is covered by the checks for an unrostered player, an out-of-position player and the weekly cap.","Rule 5.3 rewritten to say all of this, and Rule 5.3's third paragraph now states plainly that the record is the box score, not the sheet."]},{"version":"3.04","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The league now records the EA account that actually played, not just the name you typed in. A box score carries one name and one account id, and for six members the name the game reports is not the name they gave the league: one is the console persona, the other is the EA login. No amount of comparing text bridges those. The account id does. It is read first, before any name, and it is learned automatically the first time a box score finds you, so from then on your stats reach you whatever you rename yourself to and whatever is in your EA ID box. Forty-five members are already linked, and two players got tonight's goals back.","items":["Your EA account id is stored the first time a box score finds you, and matched on before any name from then on.","Two profiles cannot claim the same EA account: that is a duplicate account and it is refused rather than quietly splitting a season's stats.","Backfilled from the games already played: 45 members linked, none claimed twice.","Two box scores that matched nobody were linked where the filed lineup left only one possibility, and those players have their goals.","Seven are left for the league office, because their club had several players unaccounted for and a wrong link is worse than none.","Settings says which name the league needs, and tells you when you are linked and can stop thinking about it."]},{"version":"3.03","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"A matchup shows LIVE only while it is actually being played, and only if a player in that matchup is actually on air: from puck drop until thirty minutes later. There was no clock on it at all, so one player streaming lit every game his club played that night, including one fifty minutes away. When somebody is on air the card now carries a link straight to the stream, so a reader can watch rather than just be told. The league checks Twitch itself every two minutes, so nobody has to remember to announce it. Separately, the club crests in those cards render at twice the size they are asked for, which pushed the LIVE pill out through the side of the card; the crests are sized for that card now and the row wraps instead of overflowing.","items":["LIVE appears from a game's scheduled start until thirty minutes later, and never before puck drop.","Thirty minutes because the night's games start thirty-five apart: anything wider lights the next game's card while the current one is still on.","It appears only if a rostered player on one of those two clubs is on air, with a handle to send people to.","The card carries a Twitch link per streamer, keyboard reachable, opening in a new tab without routing you to the matchup page.","Live detection is the Twitch API, checked every two minutes, not a switch anyone has to flip.","The LIVE pill no longer hangs outside the card: the crests are sized for that card and the row wraps."]},{"version":"3.02","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"Two fixes from the first game night. Box scores were importing but no game could be marked final: an unqualified DELETE inside the playoff-clinch calculation is refused on the connections the site's API uses, and that calculation runs on a trigger when a game finals, so the write aborted after the stats had already landed. The game stayed scheduled, the import archive stayed open, and the open archive is what asked staff to enter the game by hand. Separately, a player in a box score is now matched on his EA ID before his site name, and never on his Discord name. A box-score name is an EA identity; asking the site name first put one player's night on a duplicate account.","items":["Games final automatically again. The clinch calculation cleared its working table without a WHERE clause, which the database refuses on API connections but allows in the SQL editor, so it passed every rehearsal.","Two more unqualified writes of the same kind were found and fixed before they could bite, and a test keeps the repo free of them.","Player matching now asks, in order: the EA account that played, the EA ID on the profile, the EA ID from registration, then the site name.","Discord names are no longer consulted at all. A Discord handle is not evidence about who was in an EA lobby, and it can be changed at will.","Verified against all of game night one: exactly one player's site name and EA ID disagreed, and nobody depended on a Discord name to be recognized."]},{"version":"3.00","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The box score is now checked against the position each player is rostered at, not only against whether he is on the roster at all. A club can file a legal lineup and then put a defenseman at center once the puck drops, and nothing until now looked at what actually happened. An active-roster player is held to his position group; a training-camp player fills any position, which is the rule the lineup builder has always enforced and which this mirrors rather than restates. The comparison is by group on purpose: the game reports a defenseman without saying which side he played, so a left defenseman who played defense is correct and only a real crossing of groups is flagged.","items":["An active-roster player appearing at a position outside his group is reported to the Officials' desk with what he is rostered at and what he played.","Training-camp players are exempt, exactly as they are when a lineup is filed (Rule 2.1).","By group, never by exact position: the game reports both defensemen the same way, so a left defenseman who played defense is correct.","Counted on the Staff Desk triage card alongside the other two roster flags.","Verified against a legal box score first: a defenseman recorded as the game records him, a center at wing, a goaltender in goal and a camp player anywhere all raise nothing."]},{"version":"2.99","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"Every completed game is now checked against the two clubs' rosters. If a player turns up in a box score for a club that holds him on neither its active roster nor its training camp, the department that owns it is told at once, on the site and in the staff room, with the fact that matters most: whether he is on no CGHL roster at all, or is currently rostered by another club. The same check reports a skater the league cannot identify as a member at all, which every existing check quietly skipped, so the most suspicious line in a box score was the one nobody was told about. Underneath, the last-resort name matcher treated the underscore in a gamertag as a wildcard, which could weld a stat line to the wrong member and hide exactly the thing this check is for. The same pass found that every flag this review has ever raised pointed at the public home page rather than the game, so a staffer got a notice with nothing to click through to; all five now open the box score.","items":["A player in a club's box score who is on neither its active roster nor its training camp is reported to the Transactions desk and the staff room (Rule 4.2).","The notice says whether he is on no CGHL roster at all or is currently rostered by another club, and reminds staff the roster is read as it stands now.","A skater the league cannot match to any member is reported separately: usually a missing EA ID for Statistics to link, sometimes somebody who should not have been in the lobby.","Both are raised once per game per player and survive a re-review without repeating.","The last-resort name matcher escapes the underscore, which was a wildcard by accident: Dangle_47 could match DangleX47 and put the stat line on the wrong member.","Rule 4.2 records that every completed game is checked against both rosters.","It goes to the Officials' desk, which is where Rules 5.2, 5.3 and 7.1 already send it: dressing an ineligible player is a forfeit and a Chapter 7 matter, and no other department holds either remedy.","Every flag this review raises now opens the game it is about. All five, including the three older ones, pointed at the public home page, so a staffer got a notice with nothing to click.","The Staff Desk triage card shows box scores with a player his club does not hold, over a rolling seven days."]},{"version":"2.98","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The game-night notice now says only what the league actually settled. Where a game's server had not resolved it used to name the standard server anyway, in a message that called itself final; the standard server is only the answer when neither club picked and it was not the veto, which is not this message's call to make. It says the server is still settling and points at the schedule desk instead. Rule 4.2 also said a code may be shared only with the players dressing in that game, while its own first paragraph releases it to both clubs' rosters and front offices and the club-room notice goes to the whole room; the two paragraphs agree now.","items":["An unsettled server is reported as unsettled, never guessed, and the post drops the word 'final' when one is missing.","The browser asked the resolver only for games past their own T-30, so a night's later games read 'resolving' for an hour after the database had settled them. It reads the night's lock now.","The notice names each open sheet with its own deadline in words: the 9:35 sheet until 9:05, the 10:10 sheet until 9:40.","Rule 4.2 paragraph 2 reconciled with paragraph 1: a code may be shared within the two clubs that play the game, and never outside them.","The scheduler stopped pulling every lobby code of the season into each five-minute tick. The codes come from the night board, which hands them over only once the night has locked."]},{"version":"2.97","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"Game night reaches a club in ONE message, and it waits for the night to lock before it does. It used to go out as soon as a club's first game was 75 minutes away, with the private lobby codes already in it: 45 minutes before Rule 4.2 releases them, and while the server picks were still open, so it could not name a server at all. Now it arrives at the moment the night locks and carries the whole night: every game, its time, its opponent, its settled server and its code. Underneath, the moment a night locks had been written three different ways. It is one function now, and the two that were wrong are fixed: the resolver settles every game of the night together instead of waiting for each game's own puck drop, and the picks really do freeze for the whole night, which is what the schedule desk and Rule 4.2 have always said. A night's servers are now settled by the league itself rather than waiting for a manager to open the site.","items":["The club game-night post waits for the night's lock and says everything once: matchups, times, servers, codes.","The gate is the database, not the scheduler's clock: public.night_board returns no rows until the night has locked, so an early tick has nothing to post.","public.night_lock_at is the one definition of when a night locks; can_see_match, resolve_game_server and guard_veto_deadline all read it.","resolve_game_server settled a game only at its OWN puck drop minus 30, so the second and third games of a night stayed unsettled for an hour after the desk said the night was frozen. Fixed.","guard_veto_deadline accepted a change to a late game's pick until that game's own T-30, an hour after the desk stopped offering it. Now the whole night freezes together.","The post tells the truth about Rule 5.3: at the night's lock only the first game's sheet is shut, and it names when each of the others closes.","The window closes when the night's last game starts, and a lost cron tick before that is a catch-up rather than a night with no message.","Rule 4.2 records the notice the office now owes each club, and that none of it is sent before the night locks."]},{"version":"2.96","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The league's servers are now NA Northeast, NA Southeast, NA Central and NA West, and NA East is off the list. NA Central is the standard server: a game where neither club names one is played there, and the desk says so before a club files nothing. The twelve picks that named NA East were cleared rather than moved to a server nobody chose, and both clubs were asked to pick again. Every player can record the server he plays best on in Settings, and his club's management sees the whole roster's answers on the schedule desk, beside the controls that set the picks. It is advisory: the picks and the veto stay the front office's. On each game night the office asks any club that has not filed, ninety minutes before the first puck drop and an hour before the picks freeze. Rule 4.2 now carries the procedure it was already cited for: the four servers, the standard one, how home picks and away vetoes settle a game, when they freeze, and that a player's suggestion is a suggestion.","items":["Servers: NA Northeast, NA Southeast, NA Central, NA West. NA East is retired, in the site, the pickup lobbies, the database resolver and the settings-sheet templates.","Standard server NA Central, defined once in public.default_server() and mirrored as CG.DEFAULT_SERVER. A vetoed standard server falls to the first listed server that is not the veto.","The 12 existing picks naming NA East (BOS and DAL, Wednesday and Thursday) were cleared and both clubs asked to pick again before the night's freeze.","Settings: Suggested server, one of the four or No preference. Stored on the profile, editable only by its owner.","Schedule desk: What your roster suggests, per server with counts and names, plus who has not answered.","Each game's controls now name the outcome of filing nothing before a club files nothing.","Management announcements: the nightly server-pick ask, 90 minutes before the night's first puck drop, naming only the clubs that still owe picks. A night where everyone has filed gets no post.","Rule 4.2 retitled Private game codes and server selection, with the server list, the standard server, the pick and veto procedure, the freeze and the nightly ask; standard server and veto defined in Rule 10.1."]},{"version":"2.95","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"A player is told the moment his club dresses him for a game, and told again if it takes him off that sheet; a resubmit of the same six, or a shuffle of those six between positions, notifies nobody, because nothing changed for any player. The notice names the game, its time and its lock, and opens the matchup page. The lineup builder now shows each player's availability for the week beside his name, one mark per night, so lines are built against the answers instead of in another tab. The per-game page is listed in Team HQ as Game lineups rather than being reachable only by link, and its player boxes and the builder's now look the same: position, name, rating."},{"version":"2.94","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"A game left unfinished is abandoned once either club plays a different opponent, and the league now files that itself (Rule 4.3). The club ahead when play stopped takes the win on the score as it stood, the club behind is recorded as forfeiting, and both clubs keep every statistic their players earned. Until a club moves on, an unfinished game is still treated as resumable and its sittings are added together as before. An abandoned game that was level is not ruled automatically, because no club is ahead to be given the win: the officials rule that one. Every ruling is announced to the officials and can be reversed if the clubs were in fact still going to replay."},{"version":"2.93","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"A completed game with no box score now says why. A forfeited game carries no individual statistics at all under Rule 3.2, and a final whose box score has not imported yet has none for a different reason; both used to render as a table of column headers with nothing in it. Each club's card now carries the reason in place of the empty table, and the Three Stars card carries it too rather than standing empty."},{"version":"2.92","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The overall rating on a player card in the line creator now sits on the same line as his name. It was centered against the whole card, which put it beside the position instead and crowded the longer position names."},{"version":"2.91","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"Club management sets its own players' jersey numbers, from a new column in the Team HQ roster table. A number is a whole number from one to ninety-nine, no two players on a club may wear the same one, and the league names the player already wearing it when a club tries. A number belongs to the roster spot rather than the player, so it is the club's for the season and a player who moves clubs does not carry it; a pre-season loan is not the club's to renumber. Separately, the player cards in the line creator were reformatted: the name now has its own line and the position, line chips and weekly count share the line beneath it, which stops names being cut to an initial and positions being clipped mid-word."},{"version":"2.90","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"The line creator's night plan is a row again. Each night now shows its line and a Per game button; the individual game selects appear only when that button is pressed, and a night already running more than one line opens itself so its state is never hidden behind a button. On a phone every control is full width and stacked, each tap target is a comfortable size, and the roster board drops to a single column, which stops player names being cut to a single initial on the one screen a manager builds lines on."},{"version":"2.89","dateIso":"2026-09-23","summary":"A club may now dress a different line in each game of a night, up to three. Every game in the line creator's night plan carries its own line, with an All three shortcut that sets the night at once for a club that runs the same six all evening; a game left on none is skipped and keeps whatever is already filed for it. Dressing a night, or a week, submits each game with the line set for that game. This matters because a player's week is six games and a night is three, so a club that wants everyone inside the weekly limit has to split its nights, and until now a night could only be dressed with one line."},{"version":"2.88","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A club's front office reaches its roster's availability from Team HQ. The grid of who answered what, one mark per game per night with each player's note and when it was logged, has always sat under the weekly availability page, but its menu entry hung off the permission to SUBMIT availability, which is a player's. A commissioner viewing a club's front office therefore had no way to reach it from any menu, and when he did reach it the grid read his own roster spot before the club he was viewing. Team HQ now lists Availability for anyone entitled to read a club's answers, and the grid follows the club being viewed. Players still find their own submission, and the same grid, under My Hub."},{"version":"2.87","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"The Schedule desk in Team HQ now shows the whole game week and the lineup filed for each game. It stopped after two nights, so the third night's games, their codes, their server picks and their sheets had nowhere to be read until the first night had been played; it now draws every night of the week and summarises later weeks below as before. Each game box carries the six on file with their positions, marked Set before its lock and Locked after, or Not set with the hour it is due and, once that hour has passed, what playing without a sheet costs under Rule 3.2; every box links to that game's own builder. Rostered players read the same sheets under My Hub, Lineups, which now loads the full week rather than the next six games, so the last night of a week no longer sat on Loading."},{"version":"2.86","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A commissioner previewing a club's Team HQ now sees that club's own screen and acts on the real club. The preview used to lose to a seat or a roster spot the commissioner held elsewhere, so a commissioner who still plays or manages was shown his own club's lineups, lines and trades under another club's name; an explicit preview now wins, and picking None puts it back. The preview also showed the league office's view, which is fuller than anything the club's own seats see: it can now be mirrored as the Owner, the General Manager or the Assistant General Manager, naming who holds that seat, so the pages the Owner has hidden are hidden and the pages behind approval carry their banner, exactly as that person sees them. Authority is unchanged: a commissioner's moves in a preview are real and immediate and are never queued behind an Owner, and the bar says so."},{"version":"2.85","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A filed lineup can be withdrawn. Until now every route wrote six players and nothing could unfile a game, so a club that had filled its week could not give a player his games back at all: the weekly limit counts games a club has filed, and the only way to free a player was to find a replacement who still had room. Team HQ now carries Remove lineup on the per-game builder and Clear on each night of the line creator, both before the lock only, because a locked sheet is the lineup of record (Rule 5.3). Everyone on a withdrawn sheet gets that game back in his week. A withdrawn game is unfiled, so it must be dressed again before it locks or the club is exposed under Rule 3.2, and every withdrawal is logged to the league office."},{"version":"2.84","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A line can now be dressed into some of a night's games rather than all of them. Each night in the line creator's plan lists its games, every one selected to begin with, and a club may switch any of them off so a line covers one or two games of a three-game night; a game left out keeps whatever is already filed for it. This matters because a six-game week (Rule 5.2) does not divide evenly into three-game nights. Dressing a week also no longer runs in clock order: nights that give players back are submitted before nights that take players on, so moving a player from one night to another lands in a single action instead of being refused because the game that would have freed him had not been submitted yet."},{"version":"2.83","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A player's weekly load is now visible before a line is built, not only when the league refuses one. Rule 5.2 counts the games a club has already filed as well as the games a player has actually played, which is what stops a club scheduling anyone for more than six games in a week; but nothing showed a manager that count while he was building, so a club could spend a player's whole week on two nights and discover it only when a third was refused. Every player in the line creator now wears his own figure for the week, the roster header names anyone with no games left, and the Dress the week confirmation says in advance which nights the limit will refuse and for whom. A night's refusals are grouped by reason rather than repeated once per game, and the refusal itself now says how to free a game."},{"version":"2.82","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"Lineups are set day by day. On each game day the league office asks the front offices for that night's sheets by the moment the night's first game locks, which is 8:30 PM Eastern on a nine o'clock night and moves with the night rather than being pinned to a clock, and it posts a reminder in management announcements four hours earlier naming that night's games and the sheets still to file. Nobody is asked to file a whole week at once any more; the weekly post now points at the nightly deadline instead. The lock itself is untouched: each game still locks thirty minutes before its own puck drop, so a late game stays editable at no cost long after the first one has started, and a change after a game locks is still an emergency call-up that costs one in-game minor per player changed (Rule 5.3)."},{"version":"2.81","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"The price of a post-lock change is now stated everywhere the door is offered. Rule 5.3 is unchanged and always said it: a lineup locks thirty minutes before puck drop, a change after that is an emergency call-up only, and every player changed costs the club one in-game minor served in that game, two swaps being two minors, while moving the same six players between positions costs nothing. The submit confirmation said so; the button that opens a call-up, the banner that stays on screen while it is open, the message shown when a locked sheet is edited and the league's own lineup reminder did not, and read instead as a free grace period until ten minutes after puck drop. All four now name the price."},{"version":"2.80","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"Two corrections to the weekly reminder. The office asks for a week's lineups one hour after availability closes, 8:30 PM Eastern in a normal week rather than 7:30, so that a club builds its sheets on the finished availability picture instead of racing the same clock its players are on; the lineup lock itself is unchanged and still falls thirty minutes before each game (Rule 5.3). And no post from the league's automations unfurls a link into a preview card any more: every plain-text message the site, the scheduler and the bot send now suppresses embeds, so the message is what you read rather than a site preview stacked under it. The management reminder also addresses the single CGHL Management role rather than pinging Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager separately."},{"version":"2.79","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"A reminder goes out twenty-four hours before a game week's availability window closes (Rule 5.1). Each club's room gets one post naming, by mention, the players whose answers are not in, with that club's games for the week; a club whose players have all answered is told so and nobody is pinged. The front offices get one post in management announcements, addressed to Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager, asking for that week's lineups by the same hour and counting the sheets still to file. The request is a courtesy, not a second deadline: every game still locks thirty minutes before its own puck drop, and the emergency call-up door still closes ten minutes after it (Rule 5.3). The reminder derives its hour from the week's own first game night, so a holiday-shifted week moves its reminder with it, and who has not answered is now defined once in the database for both the reminder and the nudge that follows the deadline. The league office can send or re-send a week's reminder on demand."},{"version":"2.78","dateIso":"2026-09-22","summary":"Game-night operations, audited the night before Season 1's first games. No rule changed; the software now matches the book. Lobby codes reach a tab that was already open when they release (Rule 4.2, thirty minutes before the night's first puck drop), and a club's schedule now reads that same moment instead of each game's own. The emergency call-up button disappears when the door shuts, ten minutes after puck drop (Rule 5.3), instead of offering a button the database refuses. The office's lineup readiness card refreshes itself every thirty seconds. Club game-night reminders go out up to seventy-five minutes ahead so they land before the lineups lock, voided games are not announced, and a club with no Discord room is reported instead of skipped in silence. A week's availability answers keep their nights once a night has been played. A resumed game (Rule 4.3) whose sittings add up to a tie is filed and put in front of the officials the same minute, because Rule 4.1 has no ties. Direct messages from the bot are paced and a rate-limited message is retried rather than recorded as delivered."},{"version":"2.77","dateIso":"2026-09-21","summary":"The league office can set a player's position from the Control Center: the player editor under Users and roles carries a Position field that moves his roster spot this season and his sign-up, refuses a move into a group the club's shape has no room for (Rule 2.1), logs the change, and posts it to the transaction log; his Discord position role follows."},{"version":"2.76","dateIso":"2026-09-21","summary":"Availability informs, it does not bind (Rule 5.1). Management may dress any eligible rostered player regardless of the availability he stated: the per-game lineup page no longer refuses a player marked not available, it flags him when he is placed and shows every player's answer (available, not available, no answer) on the bench; Fill from a line dresses him with a warning instead of skipping him, and Auto-fill reaches for players who said yes first, then those who did not answer, then those who said no. Players are still asked to submit availability every week; it is the club's planning tool."},{"version":"2.75","dateIso":"2026-09-20","summary":"Availability nudges (Rule 5.1). When a week's availability window closes, every rostered player whose answer is not in is told on the site and by a Discord direct message from the league bot, with what he missed and what follows; his club's room gets one line naming who is missing. The league keeps a record of every nudge, the first piece of the activity ledger."},{"version":"2.74","dateIso":"2026-09-20","summary":"Minimum service before a move (Rule 2.4). A club may not waive or trade a player who has appeared in fewer than three (3) regular-season games in the current season, on its active roster or in its training camp, so that a club plays its picks and placements before it moves them and no club acquires a player nobody has seen play. The roster page and the Trade Hub refuse the move and show the games still needed; the database refuses it too. Rules 2.2 and 2.3 refer to it; the definitions gain the term."},{"version":"2.73","dateIso":"2026-09-20","summary":"Depth placements are carried in training camp. A player placed by the league office after the draft (undrafted, or a late sign-up) joins his club's training camp rather than its active roster (0.6, 2.1, 2.8, definitions), so the active roster is the players the club drafted and its management, and stays within the published composition. Called up, a depth player takes an active-roster spot like anyone else and the roster must have room; the roster tools enforce it. Every Season 1 depth placement was moved to camp; new placements land there automatically."},{"version":"2.72","dateIso":"2026-09-20","summary":"Trades open to their numbers: every trade on the transactions desk (on the table and in the history) and every offer in a club's Trade Hub opens a detail view with each player's position, cap hit, season line and overall, and a Trade balance reading that compares the two sides in the open (a guide for the front offices and the transactions department, not a rule). The Trade Hub shows the same player lines and the balance as an offer is built. The draft room applies a pick to the club's roster and pool the instant it lands, for the front offices and the league office, with one spread-out reload for everyone when the draft completes; every draft board carries a Pos column. The active roster and training camp are two blocks on every roster list (team page, Team HQ, bench, availability grid, Control Center), camp players are marked in the directory, the stats pages and on their profile, and a Training Camp Discord role follows the camp. A club's Discord room is told when one of its players submits or changes weekly availability; trade-block listings post to #trade-block only. Private messages show the day as well as the time."},{"version":"2.71","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"Draft-night load shedding. The draft room's safety poll runs every 45 seconds for front offices and the league office and every 90 seconds for everyone else, never from a background tab; the refetch after each pick is spread over 1.5 to 9 seconds and limited to front offices and the league office, since the live feed already carries the pick to every open room. No visible change; the room stays instant with hundreds of tabs open."},{"version":"2.70","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"An expired draft clock skips the pick. The league office no longer selects from the club's board or the pool on its behalf (Rule 2.8): the pick is skipped, the club may still use it before the draft is concluded, and a pick never used is replaced after the draft by one registered player placed on the club at random, into its active roster at the league minimum, before the general depth placement. The draft desk, the draft room and the Control Center say so."},{"version":"2.69","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"The draft room's board shows one round per page. A strip of round tabs sits above the table with the round on the clock marked; the page opens on that round and follows the clock as rounds turn over, and picking another round pins the view until a Live round chip brings it back. Previous and next round buttons sit above and below the table. Nothing changed for the Control Center's draft manager, which still lists every pick with round headers."},{"version":"2.68","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"The draft room's prospect pool is searchable: a search box (gamertag, EA ID or position) and position chips sit above the list, the filter survives the room's live repaints, and each row keeps its rank in the whole pool. Draft night is easy to find for players: a large Watch the draft live button sits under the wordmark on the front page and a card tops the player dashboard from the morning of draft day; both remove themselves the moment the draft completes."},{"version":"2.67","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"The playoff eligibility floor is a season setting. Rule 8.3 no longer fixes the number of regular-season games a player needs to be dressed in the playoffs: the commissioner publishes it for each season with the roster composition and the draft round count (0.1, 2.8, 8.3, definitions), the Control Center's season settings carry the box, and the Seasons page, Team HQ's Road to card and every player's dashboard read the figure in force. The league standard drops from eighteen (18) to sixteen (16), and Season 1 is published at sixteen (16)."},{"version":"2.66","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"Management can see the club's roster spots remaining while the draft runs: a card on the Team HQ draft desk (under Board coverage) and in the draft room (after the summary tiles) shows open spots by position group against the season's shape, with the seats held by the Owner, GM and AGM counted in each group and in the header total (Rule 2.6). A group at its cap reads full, matching the greyed-out prospects in the pick dialog."},{"version":"2.65","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"Every prospect is reachable in every draft list: the Team HQ draft desk's pool, the draft room's prospect pool and the pick dialog show the whole pool in pages of 25 (they used to stop at 14, 40 and 30), and each row carries the player's EA ID. The pick dialog also searches EA IDs."},{"version":"2.64","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"The draft's round count is the commissioner's to set for each season (Rule 2.8): the Control Center's Draft manager now takes any number of rounds and records it with the board. Season 1 is built to 12 rounds, 96 picks, in the order drawn on September 10; round 1 pays $3,500,000 and round 12 the $750,000 minimum. The draft-night announcement and the on-the-clock posts now reach #announcements and #draft-hub (the default webhook they fell back to had been deleted)."},{"version":"2.63","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"Draft night: a pick that would not fit the club's shape (Rule 2.1: at most 9 forwards, 7 defensemen, 5 goaltenders, 15 in all with management) is refused with the reason instead of failing at commit; the pick dialog greys such players out; the clock's automatic pick chooses a player who fits, and skips the pick when nobody does."},{"version":"2.62","dateIso":"2026-09-19","summary":"Rule 2.8: a club drafts with its Owner and General Manager seated; the Assistant General Manager's seat may be open and filled later. Rule 2.6: management pay is Owner $0, General Manager $0, Assistant General Manager $2,000,000 (was $3,000,000 for both managers); every club's cap sheet is updated."},{"version":"2.61","dateIso":"2026-09-18","summary":"Player and club pages on phones: the header is one clean grid (crest and overall badge on the top line, the name and labels beneath), the crest is half its former size, a long gamertag no longer clips, the identity labels share one outlined style with Message as the single action, and the profile tabs fit on one row."},{"version":"2.60","dateIso":"2026-09-18","summary":"Player profile headers lose two status pills: the weekly availability chip and the season status chip (Live / not yet under way). The archived-season warning stays."},{"version":"2.59","dateIso":"2026-09-18","summary":"Every player's EA ID is shown on his public profile — rostered or not — and in the player directory, where it is also searchable (Rule 1.1). A profile with no EA ID on file says so. The database now refuses a registration without one, as the sign-up form already did."},{"version":"2.58","dateIso":"2026-09-17","summary":"The draft begins at 9:00 PM Eastern Time on its Saturday (Chapter 0, Step three) — v2.56's 9:00 AM was wrong. Season 1 drafts Saturday, September 19 at 9:00 PM ET; Season 2 on the same cadence. The Thursday 11:59 PM ET sign-up cutoff is unchanged."},{"version":"2.57","dateIso":"2026-09-17","summary":"Fixes from the September 17 stress test and permissions audit. Rule 2.2: in the basic format a club signs a waived player outright — no offer, no acceptance; clubs move players and players are not asked. Rule 4.2: private lobby codes and server picks are released thirty minutes before the night's first game, to the two clubs' rosters and front offices only, and the release is enforced in the database. Rule 5.3: an emergency substitution is accepted until ten minutes after puck drop and no later; penalties are counted per player changed; every post-lock change is reported at once to the opposing front office and the officials desk with who came out, who went in and the penalties owed, and marked on the matchup page. Rule 5.1: the system stamps every availability answer itself and records a post-deadline answer as late. Also: filed lineups are private to the two clubs until the lock; a waived or traded player leaves every filed lineup; the box score is reviewed against the filed lineup on every import, including lag-out merges; the record cannot be edited around — trade assets, roster identity and sign-up dates are pinned in the database."},{"version":"2.56","dateIso":"2026-09-17","summary":"Corrected in 2.58 — the draft is at 9:00 PM Eastern Time, not 9:00 AM. This entry had moved the draft's start from 7:00 PM to 9:00 AM on its Saturday (Chapter 0, Step three); the right time is 9:00 PM ET, Season 1 on Saturday, September 19 and Season 2 on the same cadence."},{"version":"2.55","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"Rule 5.2: a player's week is counted from the box score for every game played and from the filed lineups for every game still to come, and the lineup tools refuse to assign a player to a further game once he has reached the cap. Every box score is read against the week when a game goes final; a player over the cap, a player who appeared off the filed lineup, or a club that filed no lineup is reported to the league office automatically (Rules 5.2, 5.3). In the playoffs the four (4) game series cap of Rule 8.3 now applies when a lineup is filed, not only after the game."},{"version":"2.53","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"The banned-ability list reissued for NHL 27 (Rule 4.5). Banned at every tier: Truculence, Spark Plug, Hipster, Pressure+, Unstoppable Force and Big Rig; banned at the Red tier only: Stick ’Em Up and Big Tipper. Every pre-built loadout is banned, every special-character cosmetic is banned alongside the special-character classes, and no enforcer build may be used at either position. A player who loads into a game with a banned ability, build, loadout or cosmetic no longer forfeits the game: the game is restarted and he serves one (1) minor penalty at the start of the restarted game; a club that declines to restart forfeits (Rule 3.2). Wheels and the blanket Elite-tier ban of the NHL 26 list are withdrawn."},{"version":"2.51","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"The player-availability layout for the temporary format. Every active-roster player may be dressed in up to six (6) games a week and a training-camp player in up to three (3); the weekly availability quota of Rule 5.1 is withdrawn. In the playoffs a four (4) game cap per player per series applies to everyone, and a player must have appeared in eighteen (18) regular-season games to be dressed at all — Team HQ and each player's dashboard track the floor through the season. A roster composition may be published as complete lines plus additional players of any position (Season 1: two full lines plus three, fifteen players, management inside), and training camp is unlimited unless a limit is published (Rules 2.1, 5.1, 5.2, 8.3; Chapter 0; Rule 10.1)."},{"version":"2.50","dateIso":"2026-09-16","summary":"Season settings, and a formal register. The size and composition of the active roster (Rule 2.1) and the number of draft rounds (Rule 2.8) are no longer fixed figures in the text: the commissioner determines both for each season and publishes them before that season's draft, and the draft pay scale is stated as a formula on the $250,000 increment from the $750,000 minimum, so the book stays true whatever figures a season carries. Season 1's settings are being finalized and will be published before draft night; the figures in force appear on the Seasons page, in the draft room and in Team HQ. The basic-format chapters (0, 2.1–2.5, 2.8, 2.9, 5.2, 8.1, 8.3) are rewritten in the formal, third-person register of a league constitution — defined terms, figures with words, shall/may/must — with the explanatory passages of Chapter 0 retained as commentary. Appendix A is unchanged."},{"version":"2.48","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"The season format. The league now runs one of two formats, set per season. The BASIC format is the standard and the text of the book from Season 1: no pre-season and no appearance requirement — everyone registered by the Thursday cutoff is in the draft; a fifteen-round draft in a random snake order paying $750,000 in round fifteen up to $4,250,000 in round one; an eighteen-man roster of nine forwards, six defensemen and three goaltenders with the Owner, GM and AGM inside it; every player capped at three games a week and three games a series; a $50,000,000 hard cap; a six-week regular season with the movement deadline after week four; players-only trades; one-season contracts with no extensions and no held rights, everyone re-entering the draft each season; no free-agency week, with waived players signable from the draft's conclusion; and a six-club playoff — top three per division, the division winners resting through the opening round, three best-of-seven rounds over three weeks. The FULL format — pre-season and loans, fourteen linear rounds, 9/6/2, goaltenders six a week, $40M, eight weeks, pick trading, multi-season deals, extensions and rights, top four per division — is preserved word for word in Appendix A for when the league grows. Season 1 is re-spaced to the basic calendar: sign-ups close Thursday September 17 at 11:59 PM ET, the draft is Saturday September 19, puck drop Wednesday September 23, the deadline after week four, playoffs from November 4 (Chapters 0, 2, 5, 8; Rules 1.1, 2.1–2.5, 2.8, 2.9, 3.1, 5.2, 8.1, 8.3, 10.1)."},{"version":"2.47","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"The Discord-departure rule is back. A registered player who leaves the league Discord before being placed on a club has the sign-up withdrawn automatically after about a day's grace, archived by the league office with the player told on the site how to come back — exactly as Rule 1.1 read before v2.43 retired it. Rejoining restores nothing by itself; signing up again is a fresh registration under the normal draft-eligibility rules, and players already on a roster are never touched. Restored at the league's direction one day after it was removed (Rule 1.1, §0.3)."},{"version":"2.46","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Sign-ups stay open through the pre-season, and the draft-eligibility minimum is three games. The sign-up deadline — the draft-eligibility cutoff — is now 11:59 PM Eastern on the Thursday before the draft instead of the Monday before the pre-season, so anyone who registers during the pre-season is still in the draft pool (Rules 0.3, 1.1, 2.8; Season 1: Thursday, September 24). A randomly assigned first-year now needs three (3) pre-season appearances rather than five to remain draft-eligible, which a Thursday sign-up can still reach on the final Friday (Rules 0.4, 2.8); returning players and management stay exempt, and management's obligation to spread ice time stands. Player overalls settle in step: a rating opens at 70 and blends onto the real computed number over the first three games instead of five, and stops reading as provisional at three."},{"version":"2.45","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Club management may @ freely in its own room. A GM tagging the players he is dressing in #stars was blocked by the server's generic spam filter, which treats a mention-heavy message as spam. The four front-office roles (Owner, General Manager, Assistant General Manager and CGHL Management) are now exempt from both mention-shaped filters, and every club room is exempt from the mention-spam rule outright — a private room of about twenty, with nobody outside the club reachable from it. Re-checked on every Discord sync so it cannot drift back (Rule 1.3)."},{"version":"2.44","dateIso":"2026-09-15","summary":"Two changes for the whole roster. (1) The lineups your management sets for the week are now visible to every rostered player: a new Lineups page under My Hub lists each of the club's games this week with the six who are dressed, and the dashboard shows at a glance which games you are dressed for (Rule 5.3). The builder itself stays a Team HQ tool. (2) Weekly availability is answered per game. Each night keeps its own section with its three games, and every game gets Available or Not Available; the optional note per night stays for anything else you want your GM to know. Management's club grid shows one mark per game, and the lineup builder checks the answer for the exact game it is dressing, so an older per-night answer still counts (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.43","dateIso":"2026-09-14","summary":"Three club-facing changes. (1) A club's management can rally its room: every club role is now mentionable, so an @Club ping reaches the club's members — in the team channel and in any channel a manager can post in. Server-wide @everyone / @here pings stay reserved to the league office (Rule 1.3). (2) Leaving the Discord no longer withdraws a sign-up. Discord membership is still required to register, but a registered player keeps their place on the sign-up board and in the draft pool whether or not they stay in the server; the old automatic-withdrawal rule and its day of grace are retired (Rule 1.1, §0.3). The office keeps a manual, archived tool for genuine departures. (3) Pre-season loans are uncapped. A randomly assigned pre-season player rides the active roster as a loan and counts against neither the nine forwards / six defensemen / two goaltenders nor any roster limit, so a club can hold as many as it is assigned and every registered player gets a club for the pre-season — the assignment fills each club's open seats evenly first, then loans the rest out evenly (Rules 2.1, 0.4). Team HQ's roster page shows the loan pool as its own tally, separate from the 9/6/2 shape. (4) The weekly availability deadline moved from Sunday 8:00 PM ET to 7:30 PM ET on the day the game week starts — Wednesday in a normal week, 90 minutes before the first puck drop (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.42","dateIso":"2026-09-14","summary":"The front office hears every roster move, and pre-season loans are set apart in Team HQ. Every signing, waiver, release, trade step (offered, accepted, declined, withdrawn, reversed), contract offer and answer, pre-season loan, squad move and trade-block flag now reaches the club's whole management — Owner, GM and AGM — as a site notification and as a post in the club's private Discord room, the moment it happens. The person who made the move sees his own copy without a chime. Bulk placements (the pre-season random assignment, late sign-ups, post-draft placement, the loan release) post one summary per club naming the players. The public feeds — the transaction wire, #trade-block, #management-moves — are unchanged. On Team HQ's roster page, randomly assigned pre-season players sit in their own block below the club's contracted players, marked as loans, with the position they registered shown when they are filling another seat for the pre-season (Rule 0.4)."},{"version":"2.41","dateIso":"2026-09-13","summary":"The roster shape is by position group (Rule 2.1). A club's seventeen active players are nine forwards (centers and wings in any mix), six defensemen (either side, any mix) and two goaltenders — only the goaltender is locked to an exact position, because it is the one group a build cannot cross (Rule 4.5). A club short of left defensemen carries an extra right defenseman; a club short of centers, an extra wing. The database, the roster page, the pre-season random assignment, post-draft placement and the draft all count by group now — a club with three centers can draft a fourth to fill a wing — and a camp swap matches the group, not the exact position. Rules 0.4, 0.6, 2.8, 5.2 and 8.3 say the same."},{"version":"2.40","dateIso":"2026-09-13","summary":"Pre-season random assignment fills every open roster seat before anyone goes to camp (Rule 0.4). The league office's random assignment now works in passes across the whole sign-up pool: every player to a club with room at his registered position first; then anyone left into an open seat in his position group; then into any open seat at all; and only when every active seat in the league is filled are the rest placed in training camp, evenly and at random. Before this, a player whose position was full at every club went straight to camp while other positions sat empty — on today's pool that left six clubs a defenseman short with thirteen players unplaced. A pre-season loan out of position is for the pre-season only and does not change a player's registered position. Late sign-ups placed during the pre-season follow the same order; post-draft placement is unchanged (own position, or camp)."},{"version":"2.39","dateIso":"2026-09-12","summary":"Pre-season flexibility for management (Rules 0.4, 2.1 and 5.2). In pre-season games a club’s Owner, General Manager and Assistant General Manager may be dressed at any position — a goaltending Owner can play the wing, a defenseman GM can take center — so a club can fill its bench and get as many players into games as possible. The exception ends with the pre-season: a saved line carrying a manager out of his group dresses into pre-season games only, and the lineup builder says so. Alongside it, position groups (forwards at forward, defensemen on defense, goaltenders in goal; training-camp players anywhere) are now checked by the league’s database when a lineup or a line is filed, not only by the builder that drew it."},{"version":"2.38","dateIso":"2026-09-12","summary":"Front-office seats are the Owner’s alone, and the Owner sets each manager’s access (Rule 2.6). Only a club’s Owner may nominate a General Manager or Assistant GM, and only the Owner may remove one — at once, from Team HQ, without a league-office step; the seat falls vacant, the manager’s management contract ends with it, the move posts to the transaction wire, the removed manager is told, and the league office is notified. A GM or AGM nominates and removes nobody; the league office keeps its own power to seat and unseat. New under Team HQ → Management, for the Owner only: management permissions. For every front-office page — roster, lineup builder, schedule picks, Trade Hub, free agents, draft — each seat gets full access, Owner approves (the manager sees the page, but every move on it is queued for the Owner and made only on approval, through the usual checks), or hidden (the page is withheld and its moves refused); the game stats desk is open or hidden only, since box-score repairs are reviewed by the statistics staff. The description the Owner approves is built by the league from the move itself, never typed by the manager. The default is full access everywhere except the Management page itself, which only the Owner sees unless the Owner opens it — so nothing else changes for a club that never sets it. Approvals live on the same page, with a note either way; a manager can withdraw a queued move; a move that can no longer be made when approved fails loudly rather than half-applies. The database enforces every mode, not just the page. Every member of management — Owner, GM and AGM alike — now has the same Team HQ; before this, a manager seated or removed while signed in kept the old hub until a hard refresh, and it now follows the seat on its own."},{"version":"2.37","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"The stats import keeps to the game window and the scheduled matchup (Rule 6.2). A box score now imports automatically only when the match was played between the two clubs scheduled against each other and finished inside that fixture's game window — ten minutes before puck drop to three hours after. The poller asks EA only while a window is open and only for the clubs playing in it; the importer refuses everything else and archives it. Before this, a match between two league clubs anywhere on the calendar day (or the day either side) could be filed as their league game — a scrimmage that afternoon, a rematch after the night. The importer now also follows Rule 4.3 as written: a disconnected game's replay — the whole game, or one period — is merged into the abandoned sitting as one record, and a completed game is never resumed; on a playoff night that plays the same clubs twice, each match files on the slot it was played in. (The merge had never fired on real games: it measured EA's clock in the wrong units.) A game that finished outside its window is filed by the statistics staff from the club game stats desk."},{"version":"2.36","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"The Season 2 calendar, and the pre-season is uncapped everywhere it is described. Season 2 now exists on the site, spaced from Season 1's final playoff game (Fri Jan 15, 2027) exactly as Rule 0.2 onward describes: dark weeks Jan 16–29, owner applications by Fri Jan 22, sign-ups by Fri Jan 29 at 8:00 PM ET, pre-season Wed Feb 3 – Fri Feb 12, draft Sat Feb 13 at 7:00 PM ET, free agency Sun Feb 14 – Sun Feb 21, puck drop Wed Feb 24, eight game weeks stepping over Easter week, regular season ending Fri Apr 23, playoffs from Wed Apr 28 with the final the week of May 12. Its sign-ups open automatically at Season 1's movement deadline (Rule 1.1); until then the site, the Discord roles and every league-office routine keep treating Season 1 as the season in play — the current season is now defined once, everywhere, as the season that is active or, failing that, the earliest one not yet complete. Rule 0.4 says the pre-season opens on the first Wednesday after the sign-up deadline (it is two days later only in a first season). Rule 2.1 now says outright that the training-camp three-games-a-week cap belongs to the regular season and the playoffs; Rule 5.2 already exempted the pre-season, and the lineup tools, the line planner and Team HQ now agree with it (a goaltender may cover every line in the pre-season). Behind the site: box scores are imported from the always-on bot server, whose address EA serves, with the old Netlify poller kept as a fallback."},{"version":"2.35","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"Audit corrections. The book now says what the binding rules and the site already did: playoff series caps are three games for a skater and six for a goaltender (0.8 now matches 8.3); a trade is official the moment the second club accepts it, and the transactions department may reverse it afterward (2.3; 2.8 now cites 2.3); the pre-season opens the Wednesday after the sign-up deadline (0.4), and the pre-season table is one basis for the draft order, not the basis (0.4); registration for the next season opens automatically at the movement deadline (0.2, 0.3); how a former club exercises its rights over a restricted free agent is set by the league office before the first restricted class appears (2.2); a dead clause about a late registrant being signed through free agency is gone (0.3, 2.2); a short-handed club is refilled by automatic placement, not through a free-agent process that is closed most of the year (2.1); the loadout lists were written against NHL 26 and apply until reissued (4.5). Behind the book: pre-season loans are released when the last pre-season game closes by any result, a forfeit included, and again as a safety net the moment the draft opens; the pre-season random assignment now runs inside the database, position by position; the free-agent board lists only players whose deals have ended; the draft-night pick modal puts eligible players first; a late sign-up is labeled as awaiting placement rather than as needing pre-season games; the register page names the deadline and says what changes once it has passed; and the pre-season and draft-night Discord announcements no longer describe the abolished no-minimum and rookie-bidding rules."},{"version":"2.34","dateIso":"2026-09-11","summary":"Contract extensions and the cap year (Rules 2.2 and 2.5). A club can now re-sign a player it already holds: at any point in the final season of his deal — from the day that season's free agency opens, as in the NHL — management offers him a new contract from Team HQ, or he opens with his own number from his dashboard, and the two negotiate as in free agency. The cap year runs from one free-agency opening to the next, so an accepted extension is a signed deal for the following season: nothing changes this season, and it is checked against next season's space — every deal already signed for it plus the front office — which is the space the club will have once its expiring deals turn over. Every signing, now or for a future season, is refused outright if it would not fit; there is no exception. Team HQ now shows each club its committed money and cap space for this season and the next three, from the same arithmetic the checks use. A club keeps the exclusive right to re-sign a player whose deal just ended until the new season's free agency opens; a deal signed in that stretch is for the season under way and takes effect at once. An extension follows the player through a trade and is voided by a waiver. No club other than the one holding his deal may approach him until it has ended and free agency has opened. Until now there was no way to offer a contract to a rostered player at all, and accepting any future-season deal quietly expired the player's current one. Also: contract terms run one to three seasons everywhere — the offer forms and the database allowed four, which Rule 2.5 never did."},{"version":"2.33","dateIso":"2026-09-10","summary":"Draft and player-movement rulings. The entry draft is fourteen (14) rounds (Rule 2.8), up from ten; under the pay-by-round scale that makes round one $4,000,000 and a full class $33,250,000 — $750,000 under the cap with management seated — and the scale itself is unchanged. No club drafts without a full front office: the draft will not start while any club is missing an Owner, GM or AGM (Rules 2.6, 2.8). Rookie bidding is abolished (Rule 2.2): ten minutes after the draft concludes, every registered player still without a club — undrafted or short of five pre-season games — is placed on one by the league office at the league minimum, in random order; no club chooses, and no rookie goes to free agency (Rule 2.8). The ten-minute placement the book has promised since v2.22 is now real. Free agency is for players whose contracts have ended; a player in the final season of his deal may talk only to his own club until it opens (Rule 2.2). A drafted or placed player's first contract runs one season (Rule 2.5). The sign-up deadline is now an explicit test of draft eligibility rather than a side effect of when a player was placed (Rule 2.8). Season 1's order was drawn at random, as Rule 2.8 provides for a first season, and holds in every round. Housekeeping: a contract with no club now ends when the player is waived or a management seat is vacated, instead of lingering and mispricing a later draft pick; eighteen such contracts were closed. Separately, members can now switch the Discord account the league follows from their own settings page; site roles move at once and server roles follow."},{"version":"2.32","dateIso":"2026-09-09","summary":"Position changes now have a deadline (new Rule 2.9): 11:59 PM Eastern on the Tuesday before the first pre-season game — Tuesday September 15 for Season 1. After it, no position-change request may be filed and none may be approved; one already in the queue may still be declined so nothing is left hanging, and the commissioner keeps a narrow power to correct an office error. The deadline is derived from the published schedule rather than stored as a date, so re-spacing the pre-season moves it automatically instead of leaving a stale rule behind. Previously there was no deadline at all: a request could be filed and granted at any point in the season, which would have changed a position the pre-season assignment and every club's draft board were built on. The rule is enforced in the database on both filing and approval, and the request form now says when it closes."},{"version":"2.31","dateIso":"2026-09-09","summary":"Every salary in the league now sits on one $250,000 lattice above the $750,000 minimum (Rule 2.5). Previously only rookie bidding used that step: the rulebook named the minimum and nothing else, and the offer forms accepted any $50,000 figure, so $1,850,000 was a legal contract. The single step now governs draft pay, rookie bidding, free-agent offers, counters and revisions alike, and an off-lattice figure is refused where it is typed. A figure below the minimum used to be silently raised to it on the club's behalf; it is now refused too, so the number a club sends is always the number the player is asked to accept. Drafted players are also now paid by round rather than by pick (Rule 2.8): the last round pays the league minimum and each round above adds one step, so this season's ten-round draft runs $750,000 in round ten up to $3,000,000 in round one, every pick in a round carries the same cap hit, and a full class costs $18,750,000. The scale applies however a pick is made — by the club, by the league office, or by the clock expiring onto the club's own board. Adopted by a vote of the club owners."},{"version":"2.30","dateIso":"2026-09-02","summary":"Squad changes are unlimited (Rule 2.1): management may move a player between the active roster and training camp freely, as often as it likes, in either direction, throughout the season — including after the movement deadline, which Rule 2.4 now states explicitly never restricted these internal moves. The three-changes-a-season cap is abolished; no club had used a change when it was lifted. The active-roster shape and the three-player camp limit are unchanged and still enforced on every move. Team HQ no longer shows changes remaining or a locked squad button, and the swap picker lists every player at the position."},{"version":"2.29","dateIso":"2026-09-02","summary":"A training-camp player's salary now counts against his club's salary cap (Rules 2.1 and 2.5), reversing the exemption introduced in v2.24. Camp is unchanged in every other respect: a club may still carry three camp players beyond its seventeen active spots, they may still be dressed at any position, and they still do not consume an active roster spot. What changes is only the money — parking a salary in camp is no longer free, and the cap check at every roster move, trade and signing now counts it."},{"version":"2.28","dateIso":"2026-08-29","summary":"Free agency becomes a negotiation, as Rule 2.2 always described it: a club offers terms, the player accepts, counters, or declines, and the signing takes effect the instant he accepts — the league office confirms nothing and has no say in the move. Until now the site signed players unilaterally, first come first served, with no offer and no acceptance. The transactions department may afterwards send a completed trade back where it is not a natural hockey deal (Rule 2.4), returning every player and pick to its original club with a reason both clubs can read. The weekly appearance cap no longer applies in the pre-season (Rule 5.2): at first-season roster sizes the cap made most pre-season games impossible to ice legally, and every unfilled game is a forfeit."},{"version":"2.27","dateIso":"2026-08-25","summary":"The entry draft runs in the same order every round — the snake is abolished (Rule 2.8, Chapter 0.5). A fifth order method is added: an NHL-style draft lottery drawn from the previous season, with weighted odds for the top two picks among non-playoff clubs and playoff clubs following in reverse order of finish; a first season, having no history, uses a pure random shuffle instead. Rule 2.8 also now states what was already league practice: draft picks are tradeable assets under the ordinary trade rules, before and during the draft, with a traded pick belonging to its new club the moment the deal is accepted."},{"version":"2.26","dateIso":"2026-08-24","summary":"Two clarifications aligning the rulebook with how the league actually runs. Rule 2.6: management cap hits are Owner $0, General Manager $3,000,000, Assistant General Manager $3,000,000 — the GM line now matches the season configuration and the owners' briefing. Rule 2.1: an active player may be dressed at any position within his group (a forward at center or either wing, a defenseman at either side), never a skater in goal or a goaltender out of it, matching lineup enforcement; training-camp players remain fully flexible."},{"version":"2.25","dateIso":"2026-08-19","summary":"Rule 2.2 defines two classes of free-agent rights on the NHL's model. A player who has never completed a season on a roster is unrestricted. Once he has served a club, his rights stay with that club when the contract ends and he is a restricted free agent until he accrues four (4) off-seasons of service, after which he is unrestricted for good. Every player is unrestricted in Season 1 because the league has no service history yet. Rookie, Free Agent and Restricted Free Agent are now Discord roles, granted and revoked automatically from the same rule."},{"version":"2.24","dateIso":"2026-08-18","summary":"Rule 2.5 states plainly that a training-camp player's salary does not count against the salary cap, and Rule 2.1 says the same where camp is defined. Camp already sat outside the seventeen active spots; it now sits outside the payroll with them, and a camp player's salary begins to count only on call-up. The site had been charging camp salaries to the cap and counting camp players against the seventeen, so clubs were shown less room than they had."},{"version":"2.23","dateIso":"2026-08-18","summary":"Rule 2.6 now states plainly that a club seat is held by one person, that one person holds at most one club seat league-wide, and that an appointment through the application process into a seat that is already held is refused rather than applied. Previously such an approval overwrote the sitting manager without notice. The seat is vacated first; the league office keeps its own power to seat and unseat management directly. A nomination that cannot be seated stays open, the league office and the nominating club are told why, and the Review Board flags it before the deciding vote is cast."},{"version":"2.22","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Season 1 rules package. The pre-season now decides draft eligibility: a randomly assigned player who is not already on a club’s full-time roster must appear in at least five (5) pre-season games to stay draft-eligible, returning players and club management are exempt, and management is obligated to spread pre-season ice time so everyone who needs five games can get them. Management plays the pre-season for its own club rather than being drawn into the random assignment. Standings now run on total points earned rather than points percentage, so games in hand show in the table as they fall, and a forfeit scores for points exactly as an ordinary win or loss. Training camp is capped at three (3) players, each dressable at any position. The movement deadline lands at midnight at the end of the Friday of the deadline week. The ten-minute forfeit may now be waived, but only by a member of league staff with no involvement in the game. The banned X-Factor and build lists will be reviewed and reissued when NHL 27 releases. The playoff field expands to the top four clubs in each division, an eight-club divisional bracket."},{"version":"2.21","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Rule 1.1 closes the loop on the Discord requirement: a registered player who leaves the league Discord has their pending sign-up withdrawn automatically after about a day's grace. The registration is archived by the league office and the player is notified on the site with a path back — rejoining and signing up again is always possible and counts as a fresh registration under the normal draft-eligibility rules. Players already placed on a club are unaffected; those situations go through the league office."},{"version":"2.20","dateIso":"2026-08-12","summary":"Rule 2.6 adds the application waitlist: owner and staff applications with no open seat can be waitlisted by the league office, and when a seat opens — a resignation, a non-renewal, or a staff departure — the longest-waiting eligible application fills it automatically, in waitlist order. Eligibility is re-checked at the moment of appointment against the management-separation rule (2.7), the appointee and the commissioners are notified immediately, and the office may reverse any automatic appointment on review."},{"version":"2.19","dateIso":"2026-08-10","summary":"Rule 1.3's anti-spam gate gains a GIF carve-out: Discord's built-in GIF picker now works for everyone, including accounts that have not yet registered. Every other link from an unregistered account is refused outright — the message is blocked, which is stricter than before, when such links simply posted without a preview. Registered members are unaffected. The advertising, scam and invite filters are unchanged."},{"version":"2.18","dateIso":"2026-08-10","summary":"Rule 5.3 now prices post-lock lineup changes: one (1) in-game penalty per player changed after the thirty-minute lock, counted per submission, recorded automatically, served as minor penalties in that game and verified by the officials against the box score. Going unserved is reviewed under Chapter 7. The emergency-substitution allowance itself is unchanged — this sets its cost, so a genuine no-show swap is one penalty and wholesale post-lock rebuilds price themselves out."},{"version":"2.17","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 1.3 records #scouting-links, the club recruitment board: the whole league can read it, and only a club's Owner, General Manager, or Assistant General Manager may post in it. Clubs post their own Discord there for recruiting; the channel is exempt from the invite-link and advertising filters because ordinary recruiting copy trips them, and because the set of people who can write in it is already the narrowest on the server."},{"version":"2.16","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 1.3 corrected on server invite links. They were blocked outright for everyone except the Commissioner and Staff, which caught club owners sharing their own team's Discord — a normal and useful thing to post. Invite links now follow the same line as every other link: sign up and you may post one; an account that has not signed up may not. Advertising and scam content are still blocked automatically for everyone, as is mention spam."},{"version":"2.15","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 3.1 now states the season length the league actually plays: eight (8) game-weeks and seventy-two (72) games per club, up from the six (6) and fifty-four (54) it had carried since v2.6. The schedule had been built to eight weeks for some time — this corrects the rulebook to match the ice, it does not change anyone's season. Rule 3.1 also now says plainly that a week skipped for an observed holiday does not shorten the season: the schedule runs a week later and the game count still holds. The figure on the Puck drop timeline is now read from the season itself rather than restated, so it cannot drift from the schedule again."},{"version":"2.14","dateIso":"2026-08-09","summary":"Rule 3.1 and the orientation chapter now state when the playoffs open: the game week immediately after the final regular-season week. This was always the intent but was never written down, and nothing enforced it — a season whose weeks shifted because an observed holiday was skipped kept its original playoff date, which put the last regular-season week on top of the postseason. Generating the regular season now sets the playoff date from the weeks it actually laid down, and the Control Center flags the two drifting apart."},{"version":"2.13","dateIso":"2026-08-08","summary":"Rule 1.3 records the Discord anti-spam measures: posting links, images, or attachments now requires a league role — register on the site and it unlocks within seconds — new accounts must be in the server ten minutes before posting at all, and invites, advertising, and scam content are blocked automatically. Reading and chatting are unchanged."},{"version":"2.12","dateIso":"2026-08-06","summary":"Chapter 7 now states who holds which disciplinary power, matching what the system already enforces: on-ice complaints and appeals route to the officiating department, Discord-conduct complaints to the community department (warnings, escalate the rest); staff suspensions cap at 10 games or 30 days, and season-long suspension, expulsion, or any sanction against a commissioner or staff member is a commissioner's alone."},{"version":"2.11","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 1.3 tightened: server-wide pings follow the Commissioner and Staff roles only. The Media role had been left able to send them on the reasoning that media-only staff do not wear the Staff role — which meant someone carrying neither Commissioner nor Staff could ping the whole server, the exact thing the rule forbids. Media no longer carries it; a staffer whose only role is their department asks the office to post."},{"version":"2.10","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 7.1 now states how a complaint travels: it reaches the department that must act on it, and that department is told the moment it is filed. Every official can see every filing, including one about a commissioner or a staff member; what is restricted is authority, not visibility — no official rules on a case they filed or a case about themselves. The substance of a complaint is never posted to a staff channel, only that one was filed and under which heading."},{"version":"2.9","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Rule 1.3: @everyone and @here are now reserved to commissioners and league staff. The permission had been granted to every position role, every club-seat role, every staff department role and the server-booster role — which in practice meant every registered player could ping the entire server. It has been removed from all of them and is re-enforced on every Discord sync, so it cannot drift back."},{"version":"2.8","dateIso":"2026-08-05","summary":"Game-night law, in one pass. Late starts (Rule 3.2): one penalty at five minutes, two at eight — the ready club choosing a 5-on-3 or two 5-on-4s — and a hard, unwaivable forfeit at ten; the penalties themselves may be waived by mutual agreement, and matchmaking delay from the previous game is exempt. Disconnections (Rule 4.3) rewritten: the dropped club leaves at the next whistle with everything before it counting, reloads with a penalty timed to the drop, doubles it on a second, and a third ends the game as a forfeit loss that KEEPS its statistics and publishes as an FFL even if the forfeiting club scored more; third-period drops take the penalty five minutes earlier, and a drop in the first five minutes of the third replays one period as the third. Weekly caps corrected to the enforced numbers (Rule 5.2): skaters three, goaltenders six, camp three at any position, rostered players taking precedence. Playoffs (Rule 8.3): best-of-seven in a 2-2-3 week, first to four advancing, the weekly cap serving as the series cap, and the Rule 2.4 emergency goaltender expressly playoff-eligible. Rule 4.1 gains the lobby side-selection rule and the 20-minute clock-display note. Registration (Rule 1.1): players may withdraw their own sign-up until registration ends, and the next season's registration opens automatically when the movement deadline passes. The rulebook file itself was consolidated from its amendment chain into a single current text."},{"version":"2.7","dateIso":"2026-08-03","summary":"The roster rework. The active roster is now a fixed seventeen — 3 centers, 3 left wings, 3 right wings, 3 left defensemen, 3 right defensemen, 2 goaltenders — and training camp is unlimited (Rule 2.1). Weekly game caps arrive: camp players dress in up to 3 games a week at any position, active players up to 6, goaltenders up to 9 (Rule 5.2). Every minimum-games rule is abolished — the five-game draft threshold (Rule 2.8) and the 30% playoff floor (Rule 8.3) are gone; every registered player is draft-eligible and every rostered player is playoff-eligible. Random placement of new players now closes at the movement deadline (Rules 2.2, 2.4). The playoff series cap is restated: four games per series for skaters, with goaltenders exempt and able to play all seven if needed (Rule 8.3)."},{"version":"2.6","dateIso":"2026-08-02","summary":"The playing week is now three nights — Wednesday, Thursday and Friday — three games a night, nine games a week, for the same fifty-four games per club over six game-weeks instead of nine (Rule 3.1, Chapter 0). The weekly availability floor scales with it: six of the week’s nine games including all three games of at least two nights, up from four of six including one night (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"2.5","dateIso":"2026-07-30","summary":"Rule 2.7 tightened again: Media holds NO ballot. Not on staff votes of any kind, not on rule changes, and not on season awards — previously they could still vote on anything scoped to media. A vote can bend toward the club a media staffer runs, and Media is the one department allowed to run a club, so the department now reports on the league rather than deciding for it. Media staff still read every tally. Commissioners are unaffected. Enforced on the staff vote, the award ballot, and the eligible-voter count so turnout can't be skewed by a ballot nobody can cast."},{"version":"2.4","dateIso":"2026-07-29","summary":"Rule 2.7 tightened: Media is an exclusive staff department. Someone on Media holds Media and nothing else, and votes only on media topics — not on whole-office questions. In exchange Media stays the one department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM or AGM seat, because a media staffer decides nothing about any club. Want a second department? You give up Media and the club seat. Commissioners are exempt throughout — the office carries every department and every vote. Enforced by the site at every write path, including the staff vote."},{"version":"2.3","dateIso":"2026-07-29","summary":"Rule 2.7 amended: Media is the one staff department that may also hold a club's Owner, GM, or AGM seat. Media rules on nothing — no disputes, discipline, applications, officiating, transactions or draft — so a media staffer running a club has no decision to make about their own team. The exception is Media-alone: taking on a second department means giving up the club seat, a seated member joining the staff may be given Media and nothing else, and it never applies to commissioners. Enforced by the site, both directions."},{"version":"2.2","dateIso":"2026-07-24","summary":"Rookie free agency runs as a live auction (Rule 2.2): bids on an undrafted five-game rookie start at $750K and rise in $250K increments, and every bid resets a 12-hour clock — the high bid when the clock expires wins, and the bid amount becomes the contract (previously only the first bid started the clock). Open free agency is clarified: for every other available player, clubs send custom-salary offers and the player chooses, expected in good faith to take the strongest offer — signing a lower one (to play with friends) isn’t forbidden but isn’t to be abused."},{"version":"2.1","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Terminology corrected across the rulebook: the rules referred to a club “captain,” a role the league does not use. A club is run by its management — Owner, General Manager, and Assistant GM (Rule 2.6) — so protests and paperwork (1.3), trades (2.3), reschedules (3.3), game-code release (4.2), lobby-failure evidence (4.3), availability (5.1), discipline (7.1, 7.4), and award voting (9.2) now cite club management, and the definitions (10.1) replace “captain” with “club management.”"},{"version":"2.0","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"New Rule 4.5 — Positional builds and X-Factor restrictions. To keep games decided by skill rather than loadout, the league bans a defined set of X-Factor traits (Truculence, Big Tipper, Hipster, Pressure+, Spark Plug, Unstoppable, Big Rig, Wheels) and every Elite-tier trait, bars all special-character player classes and special or mascot characters, and locks each position to a legal set of builds: forwards may not use the Enforcer build (or any defenseman build), and defensemen may not use the Enforcer Defenseman build (or any forward build) — Power Forward and Defensive Defenseman are permitted. Icing a banned build or ability makes the lineup ineligible and forfeits the game (Rule 5.2). The full list is maintained by the league office with the settings sheet (Rule 4.1)."},{"version":"1.9","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Rule 2.7 corrected: a commissioner may serve as league staff — the two offices work together. The separation that still holds is between the league office (commissioners and staff) and a club's front office (Owner, GM, AGM): no one holds a seat in both at the same time, in either direction. The staff application reflects this — anyone currently owning or managing a club must step down from that seat before joining the staff, and vice versa."},{"version":"1.8","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"The salary cap is stated as what it is — a season setting the commissioner controls — and Rule 2.5 now shows the live figure for the current season rather than a number frozen into the text. Rule 2.1 gains the operative form of the training-camp game cap: because the two game nights together run past three games, a camp player may be dressed on only one of them, and the lineup builder now enforces it."},{"version":"1.7","dateIso":"2026-07-23","summary":"Roster structure fixed at fifteen players — a twelve-player pro roster of 2 goaltenders, 4 defensemen and 6 forwards, plus 3 training-camp players (Rule 2.1). Position assignments are now binding: defensemen play defense and forwards play forward, and only a training-camp player may fill any position. Training-camp players are capped at 3 games a week (Rules 2.1, 5.2), and a player may swap between the pro roster and training camp no more than 3 times a season — all of it enforced in Team HQ. The entry draft is fixed at ten rounds (Rule 2.8). Free agency now runs on two tracks (Rule 2.2): undrafted rookies who met the five-game pre-season minimum are bid on, with a 12-hour countdown starting at the first bid, while returning players whose contracts expired negotiate open re-signings with club management."},{"version":"1.6","dateIso":"2026-07-19","summary":"Season 1 will be played on EA Sports NHL 27, which releases September 11, 2026 — ahead of the September 16 pre-season and the October 7 puck drop. Rule 4.1 and the site now name NHL 27 as the official title; the game settings themselves are unchanged (World of Chel 6v6: three 4-minute periods, continuous full-length 5-on-5 sudden-death overtime, no shootout)."},{"version":"1.5","dateIso":"2026-07-18","summary":"Annual registration made universal: a contract does not replace signing up (Rule 2.5). A contracted player who does not register cannot play, while the full salary counts against the club's cap as dead money; if the club's ownership changes while the player is still unregistered past the sign-up deadline, the contract is voided and the player is suspended through the deal's final season. Registering at any time returns a contracted player directly to their club at no net cap change. Automatic placement explicitly never applies to contracted, suspended, or banned players (Rule 2.2); the season walk-through was updated to match (Chapter 0.3, 0.9)."},{"version":"1.4","dateIso":"2026-07-18","summary":"Game settings updated to World of Chel: three 4-minute periods with continuous, full-length 5-on-5 sudden-death overtime and no shootout (Rule 4.1; playoffs conformed in Rule 8.3). Added Chapter 0, a full step-by-step walk-through of how the season works and why. Codified the entry draft (Rule 2.8), the separation of league staff and commissioners from club management (Rule 2.7), and automatic placement of unrostered registrants onto short-handed clubs (Rule 2.2, reconciled with the movement deadline in Rule 2.4). Corrected the season length to nine game-weeks / 54 games (Rule 3.1), expanded the disconnect procedure (Rule 4.3), and set a weekly availability floor (Rule 5.1)."},{"version":"1.3","dateIso":"2026-07-15","summary":"Chapter 2 expanded to codify the salary cap and contract system (Rule 2.5) and the Owner/GM/AGM management structure (Rule 2.6): the $65M hard cap, one-to-three-season contracts, the $750K minimum, waiver salary reinstatement, $0 Owner/GM contracts, and the $3M assistant-manager contract. Seeding now states the points-percentage standard (Rule 8.1)."},{"version":"1.2","dateIso":"2026-07-01","summary":"Overtime format changed to a 5-minute 3-on-3 sudden-death period followed by a shootout (Rule 4.1). Rule 7.4 (Dangerous contact) strengthened: the 2-game baseline suspension is now mandatory for verified violations and may not be resolved by warning alone."},{"version":"1.1","dateIso":"2026-06-10","summary":"Clarified disconnect and rejoin procedure (Rule 4.3): individual drops play on with rejoin at the next stoppage; full lobby failures resume from the recorded score and clock where feasible."},{"version":"1.0","dateIso":"2026-05-20","summary":"Initial publication of the CGHL Official Rulebook for Season 1."}]},"rankings":{"intro":"","entries":[]},"ops":{"complaints":[]},"awards":{"threeStars":[],"potw":[]}};
